    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "annot-cofre-module-20260727",
      "x": 70,
      "y": 4424.5,
      "width": 980,
      "height": 520,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-07-27 (NEW MODULE api/src/cofre/ - WS-B/WS-C credential custody).\nSecond new top-level module of this run, registered in the same .eslintrc.cjs zone array as\napi/src/security/. Imports data/ (store, scoped, crypto) and security/ (origin-binding); it is\nimported BY integrations/, automation/ (through an injected seam), bridge/ and routes/. It must\nNOT live under data/ - a policy decision does not belong in the storage tier.\n\nservice.ts holds THE seam: unwrap(itemId, actor, usageContext, opts) is the one function in the\ncodebase that turns a credential REFERENCE into a value. Fail-closed on four independent grounds,\nchecked in this order and ALL before anything decrypts: (1) tenancy - items are OWNER-scoped via\nOwnerVisibilityScoped, and a row outside scope reads as absent (uniform NOT_FOUND, never 403, so\nthe seam is not an existence oracle); (2) an active GRANT - default deny, per-credential, with no\nvault-wide unlock to fall back on, and a this_run grant consumed by RUN IDENTITY rather than by a\nclock; (3) ORIGIN BINDING (I6) through security/origin-binding.ts, against the item's own\nboundOrigins; (4) existence. Only then does decrypt() run.\n\nWHY ONE FUNCTION: three later pieces install here with zero call-site churn - WS-K swaps decrypt()\nfor a per-tenant DEK wrapped by a Cloud KMS key, the parked passkey-PRF crypto-lock becomes an\nadditional precondition inside the same function, and the Registo 'item used' event has exactly one\nplace to fire from. If any workstream bypasses unwrap(), WS-K becomes a repo-wide refactor.\n\nstore.ts makes FIXED-14's claim true for this module: items and grants are reachable ONLY through\nthe scoped repositories, and the raw handles are not exported. items.ts GENERALIZES the shipped\ngateway-keys lifecycle (auth/gateway-keys-service.ts) rather than building a second one beside it.\n\nHONESTY: this is a POLICY lock. Cortex can decrypt an item whenever a grant exists, so Ekoa is a\ntrusted custodian, NOT zero-knowledge, and the product copy must never say otherwise.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-07-27 (NEW MODULE api/src/cofre/ - WS-B/WS-C credential custody).\nSecond new top-level module of this run, registered in the same .eslintrc.cjs zone array as\napi/src/security/. Imports data/ (store, scoped, crypto) and security/ (origin-binding); it is\nimported BY integrations/, automation/ (through an injected seam), bridge/ and routes/. It must\nNOT live under data/ - a policy decision does not belong in the storage tier.\n\nservice.ts holds THE seam: unwrap(itemId, actor, usageContext, opts) is the one function in the\ncodebase that turns a credential REFERENCE into a value. Fail-closed on four independent grounds,\nchecked in this order and ALL before anything decrypts: (1) tenancy - items are OWNER-scoped via\nOwnerVisibilityScoped, and a row outside scope reads as absent (uniform NOT_FOUND, never 403, so\nthe seam is not an existence oracle); (2) an active GRANT - default deny, per-credential, with no\nvault-wide unlock to fall back on, and a this_run grant consumed by RUN IDENTITY rather than by a\nclock; (3) ORIGIN BINDING (I6) through security/origin-binding.ts, against the item's own\nboundOrigins; (4) existence. Only then does decrypt() run.\n\nWHY ONE FUNCTION: three later pieces install here with zero call-site churn - WS-K swaps decrypt()\nfor a per-tenant DEK wrapped by a Cloud KMS key, the parked passkey-PRF crypto-lock becomes an\nadditional precondition inside the same function, and the Registo 'item used' event has exactly one\nplace to fire from. If any workstream bypasses unwrap(), WS-K becomes a repo-wide refactor.\n\nstore.ts makes FIXED-14's claim true for this module: items and grants are reachable ONLY through\nthe scoped repositories, and the raw handles are not exported. items.ts GENERALIZES the shipped\ngateway-keys lifecycle (auth/gateway-keys-service.ts) rather than building a second one beside it.\n\nHONESTY: this is a POLICY lock. Cortex can decrypt an item whenever a grant exists, so Ekoa is a\ntrusted custodian, NOT zero-knowledge, and the product copy must never say otherwise.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-07-27 (NEW MODULE api/src/cofre/ - WS-B/WS-C credential custody).\nSecond new top-level module of this run, registered in the same .eslintrc.cjs zone array as\napi/src/security/. Imports data/ (store, scoped, crypto) and security/ (origin-binding); it is\nimported BY integrations/, automation/ (through an injected seam), bridge/ and routes/. It must\nNOT live under data/ - a policy decision does not belong in the storage tier.\n\nservice.ts holds THE seam: unwrap(itemId, actor, usageContext, opts) is the one function in the\ncodebase that turns a credential REFERENCE into a value. Fail-closed on four independent grounds,\nchecked in this order and ALL before anything decrypts: (1) tenancy - items are OWNER-scoped via\nOwnerVisibilityScoped, and a row outside scope reads as absent (uniform NOT_FOUND, never 403, so\nthe seam is not an existence oracle); (2) an active GRANT - default deny, per-credential, with no\nvault-wide unlock to fall back on, and a this_run grant consumed by RUN IDENTITY rather than by a\nclock; (3) ORIGIN BINDING (I6) through security/origin-binding.ts, against the item's own\nboundOrigins; (4) existence. Only then does decrypt() run.\n\nWHY ONE FUNCTION: three later pieces install here with zero call-site churn - WS-K swaps decrypt()\nfor a per-tenant DEK wrapped by a Cloud KMS key, the parked passkey-PRF crypto-lock becomes an\nadditional precondition inside the same function, and the Registo 'item used' event has exactly one\nplace to fire from. If any workstream bypasses unwrap(), WS-K becomes a repo-wide refactor.\n\nstore.ts makes FIXED-14's claim true for this module: items and grants are reachable ONLY through\nthe scoped repositories, and the raw handles are not exported. items.ts GENERALIZES the shipped\ngateway-keys lifecycle (auth/gateway-keys-service.ts) rather than building a second one beside it.\n\nHONESTY: this is a POLICY lock. Cortex can decrypt an item whenever a grant exists, so Ekoa is a\ntrusted custodian, NOT zero-knowledge, and the product copy must never say otherwise.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 3,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "strokeColor": "#b45309",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
