    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 509952808,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "asbuilt-20260803-d3-achieve",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 60,
      "y": 11508,
      "strokeColor": "#0e7490",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 1030,
      "height": 665,
      "seed": 134983644,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1754222000000,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 3,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-03 (run 20260801-171149, slice D3) - `achieve`: THE PLATFORM EXTENDS ITSELF.\n\nTWO NEW MODULES, both tier 3 (integrations/):\n  api/src/integrations/integration-achieve.ts  - the FLOW: match -> execute, or draft -> verify ->\n      persist. Imports integration-capability (the ONE execute rail + the ONE resolution),\n      definition-registry, definition-store, credential-cofre (the egress binding), authored-action,\n      billing/ (the allowance gate before a model call) and llm/ for decideForTask + the attribution\n      types. It does NOT import agents/ - see the seam below - and it does NOT import action-executor\n      or action-consent: it reaches the executor ONLY through executeIntegrationCapabilityAction, so\n      the rail count is unchanged at three.\n  api/src/integrations/authored-action.ts      - the GUARDRAILS as pure functions: the deterministic\n      verification suite and the provisional/trusted state machine. Imports definitions (the canonical\n      shape + the repo's own secret scrub), action-consent's actionShape, security/origin-binding.\n\nNEW SEAM, and the reason it is a seam: the drafting turn runs on D2's shared authoring core\n(agents/authoring-core.ts), which is TIER 5. integrations/ is tier 3 and may not import it, so it\narrives as a typed callback - ActionDrafter - bound ONCE in server.ts with\n`(input) => authorWithRepair({ ...input, emptyReply: 'unavailable' })` and threaded through the\nintegrations router beside runAutomationBackedAction. That binding is ONE LINE on purpose: the prompt,\nthe parser, the guardrail suite, the fork rule and the persistence all live in the module, so nothing\nthat decides what gets STORED sits in a lambda at the composition root (the A2 review's dead-code\nclass). Absent the seam, achieve still EXECUTES and refuses to author with a coded\n`authoring_unavailable` - an honest default, never a fallback into a second authoring path (Rule 1).\n\nROUTES: routes/integrations.ts keeps its two admission tiers and gains one route in each.\n  TIER 1 (requireUserOrApiKey, above the blanket):  POST /:key/achieve\n  TIER 2 (below r.use(requireAuth)):                POST /:key/actions/:actionName/trust\nThat split IS the security argument. achieve is the capability an agent calls; `trust` - the human\npromotion that un-gates an authored action - is auth:'user' for exactly the reason C2's three consent\ndescriptors are, and C3's lessons pair is: a gate that grants its own exemption is not a gate.\n\nCOHERENCE FIX IN THE SAME SLICE (routed here by the 2026-08-03 credential fix):\nintegration-capability.ts resolved its CATALOG as the READER while EXECUTION resolves as the\ncredential's CUSTODIAN, so a peer's catalog could list their own private package's actions while the\nnext execute ran the custodian's. Both now come from ONE primitive, resolveCapabilityDefinition, in\nthe executor's own order: config row first, definitionActorForCredential from it, resolve as that\nprincipal. NEW EDGE: integration-capability.ts -> credential-cofre.ts (the custodian rule, imported,\nnever re-derived).",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-03 (run 20260801-171149, slice D3) - `achieve`: THE PLATFORM EXTENDS ITSELF.\n\nTWO NEW MODULES, both tier 3 (integrations/):\n  api/src/integrations/integration-achieve.ts  - the FLOW: match -> execute, or draft -> verify ->\n      persist. Imports integration-capability (the ONE execute rail + the ONE resolution),\n      definition-registry, definition-store, credential-cofre (the egress binding), authored-action,\n      billing/ (the allowance gate before a model call) and llm/ for decideForTask + the attribution\n      types. It does NOT import agents/ - see the seam below - and it does NOT import action-executor\n      or action-consent: it reaches the executor ONLY through executeIntegrationCapabilityAction, so\n      the rail count is unchanged at three.\n  api/src/integrations/authored-action.ts      - the GUARDRAILS as pure functions: the deterministic\n      verification suite and the provisional/trusted state machine. Imports definitions (the canonical\n      shape + the repo's own secret scrub), action-consent's actionShape, security/origin-binding.\n\nNEW SEAM, and the reason it is a seam: the drafting turn runs on D2's shared authoring core\n(agents/authoring-core.ts), which is TIER 5. integrations/ is tier 3 and may not import it, so it\narrives as a typed callback - ActionDrafter - bound ONCE in server.ts with\n`(input) => authorWithRepair({ ...input, emptyReply: 'unavailable' })` and threaded through the\nintegrations router beside runAutomationBackedAction. That binding is ONE LINE on purpose: the prompt,\nthe parser, the guardrail suite, the fork rule and the persistence all live in the module, so nothing\nthat decides what gets STORED sits in a lambda at the composition root (the A2 review's dead-code\nclass). Absent the seam, achieve still EXECUTES and refuses to author with a coded\n`authoring_unavailable` - an honest default, never a fallback into a second authoring path (Rule 1).\n\nROUTES: routes/integrations.ts keeps its two admission tiers and gains one route in each.\n  TIER 1 (requireUserOrApiKey, above the blanket):  POST /:key/achieve\n  TIER 2 (below r.use(requireAuth)):                POST /:key/actions/:actionName/trust\nThat split IS the security argument. achieve is the capability an agent calls; `trust` - the human\npromotion that un-gates an authored action - is auth:'user' for exactly the reason C2's three consent\ndescriptors are, and C3's lessons pair is: a gate that grants its own exemption is not a gate.\n\nCOHERENCE FIX IN THE SAME SLICE (routed here by the 2026-08-03 credential fix):\nintegration-capability.ts resolved its CATALOG as the READER while EXECUTION resolves as the\ncredential's CUSTODIAN, so a peer's catalog could list their own private package's actions while the\nnext execute ran the custodian's. Both now come from ONE primitive, resolveCapabilityDefinition, in\nthe executor's own order: config row first, definitionActorForCredential from it, resolve as that\nprincipal. NEW EDGE: integration-capability.ts -> credential-cofre.ts (the custodian rule, imported,\nnever re-derived).",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-03 (run 20260801-171149, slice D3) - `achieve`: THE PLATFORM EXTENDS ITSELF.\n\nTWO NEW MODULES, both tier 3 (integrations/):\n  api/src/integrations/integration-achieve.ts  - the FLOW: match -> execute, or draft -> verify ->\n      persist. Imports integration-capability (the ONE execute rail + the ONE resolution),\n      definition-registry, definition-store, credential-cofre (the egress binding), authored-action,\n      billing/ (the allowance gate before a model call) and llm/ for decideForTask + the attribution\n      types. It does NOT import agents/ - see the seam below - and it does NOT import action-executor\n      or action-consent: it reaches the executor ONLY through executeIntegrationCapabilityAction, so\n      the rail count is unchanged at three.\n  api/src/integrations/authored-action.ts      - the GUARDRAILS as pure functions: the deterministic\n      verification suite and the provisional/trusted state machine. Imports definitions (the canonical\n      shape + the repo's own secret scrub), action-consent's actionShape, security/origin-binding.\n\nNEW SEAM, and the reason it is a seam: the drafting turn runs on D2's shared authoring core\n(agents/authoring-core.ts), which is TIER 5. integrations/ is tier 3 and may not import it, so it\narrives as a typed callback - ActionDrafter - bound ONCE in server.ts with\n`(input) => authorWithRepair({ ...input, emptyReply: 'unavailable' })` and threaded through the\nintegrations router beside runAutomationBackedAction. That binding is ONE LINE on purpose: the prompt,\nthe parser, the guardrail suite, the fork rule and the persistence all live in the module, so nothing\nthat decides what gets STORED sits in a lambda at the composition root (the A2 review's dead-code\nclass). Absent the seam, achieve still EXECUTES and refuses to author with a coded\n`authoring_unavailable` - an honest default, never a fallback into a second authoring path (Rule 1).\n\nROUTES: routes/integrations.ts keeps its two admission tiers and gains one route in each.\n  TIER 1 (requireUserOrApiKey, above the blanket):  POST /:key/achieve\n  TIER 2 (below r.use(requireAuth)):                POST /:key/actions/:actionName/trust\nThat split IS the security argument. achieve is the capability an agent calls; `trust` - the human\npromotion that un-gates an authored action - is auth:'user' for exactly the reason C2's three consent\ndescriptors are, and C3's lessons pair is: a gate that grants its own exemption is not a gate.\n\nCOHERENCE FIX IN THE SAME SLICE (routed here by the 2026-08-03 credential fix):\nintegration-capability.ts resolved its CATALOG as the READER while EXECUTION resolves as the\ncredential's CUSTODIAN, so a peer's catalog could list their own private package's actions while the\nnext execute ran the custodian's. Both now come from ONE primitive, resolveCapabilityDefinition, in\nthe executor's own order: config row first, definitionActorForCredential from it, resolve as that\nprincipal. NEW EDGE: integration-capability.ts -> credential-cofre.ts (the custodian rule, imported,\nnever re-derived).",
