      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 locality, rounds 1-5: the module, the seam, the deep import, and the\ncorrection to the origin-posture note above).\n\nautomation/locality.ts - NEW tier-5 module inside automation/, imported by engine.ts. PURE by\nconstruction: no store, no seam, no env var, every input an argument - which is what makes its whole\ndecision table drivable from a test, including the cross-tenant cases. It folds the step's\ndeclaration, the origin's POSTURE (origin-posture.ts) and the fleet's answer (egress-policy.ts) into\none verdict - bridge / in-process / blocked - and answers a second question beside it:\nhostedTypistPermitFor, whether the hosted typist may type this step's password AND THROUGH WHICH\nDOOR. One rule: the login leaves by the same door as the work; when it cannot, the permit is\nWITHHELD and the run halts asking for a person, which is the closed answer and an available one.\nNO NEW MODULE EDGE - it reads posture through origin-posture.ts, which already reads the\nintegrations/ declaration through a STRUCTURAL type (the schedules/supervisor.ts pattern), so\nautomation/ still reaches integrations/ only through seams wired at the composition root.\n\nCORRECTION TO annot-automation-primitives-20260818 (above), WHICH THIS FALSIFIES. That note ends\n\"NO consumer wired yet - P4 (egress opt-in + bridge-only routing) and P3.3 (typist vs attended\nre-auth) consume it rather than re-deciding\". P4 IS THAT CONSUMER AND IT IS NOW WIRED:\norigin-posture.ts is read by locality.ts (where the step runs), by credential-gate.ts (whether the\nhosted typist may be reached at all, and whether a ceremony machine becomes a preference - it does\nfor adversarial origins only), and by session-establishment.ts (requiresAttendedAuth). The\nDISCOVERY_BUDGET half of that same note is still unconsumed; only the posture sentence is stale.\n\nNEW SEAM: EgressCandidateResolver (automation/seams.ts), bound at the composition root to\nbridge/registry.ts egressCandidatesForOrg. Direction is the usual one - automation/ never imports\nbridge/, and server.ts is the only place the two meet. DEFAULT DENY: the unbound resolver answers\n[], which REFUSES rather than widening egress, so a half-wired process cannot route a run anywhere\nnew. Org-scoped by construction (Rule 5): a machine in another tenant is not a candidate at all\nrather than one filtered later. Isolation suite: tests/security/locality-isolation.test.ts.\n\nNEW DEEP IMPORT: engine.ts now imports egress-policy.ts VALUES (residentialEgressPairings,\nmachineRetired), not only its types, and locality.ts's SESSION_MACHINE_RETIRED_REASON. Both are\ninside automation/, so no tier boundary moves.\n\nROUND FIVE, AND WHY IT IS A STRUCTURAL NOTE RATHER THAN A FIX LOG. The run loop never passed\nresidentialAvailable to the credential gate, so checkoutSession received [] and refused EVERY\nattended session - making the entire P4.2 ceremony-preference path unreachable in production. The\nfleet fact now flows engine.ts -> credential-gate.ts -> session-establishment.ts ->\ncofre/session-checkout.ts, and it is derived by the SAME predicate egress selection uses\n(residentialEgressPairings, egress-policy.ts). That shared predicate is the structural point: \"this\nmachine may carry the work\" and \"this machine's session may be released\" are two answers about one\nmachine, and two copies of the filter would let them drift apart silently, in the direction where a\nsession is unwrapped for a route that does not exist. machineRetired is the second shared predicate,\nasked by locality.ts (about a learned preference) and by engine.ts (about the machine checkout\nnamed), so a retired ceremony machine produces one halt whichever side reaches it first.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 locality, rounds 1-5: the module, the seam, the deep import, and the\ncorrection to the origin-posture note above).\n\nautomation/locality.ts - NEW tier-5 module inside automation/, imported by engine.ts. PURE by\nconstruction: no store, no seam, no env var, every input an argument - which is what makes its whole\ndecision table drivable from a test, including the cross-tenant cases. It folds the step's\ndeclaration, the origin's POSTURE (origin-posture.ts) and the fleet's answer (egress-policy.ts) into\none verdict - bridge / in-process / blocked - and answers a second question beside it:\nhostedTypistPermitFor, whether the hosted typist may type this step's password AND THROUGH WHICH\nDOOR. One rule: the login leaves by the same door as the work; when it cannot, the permit is\nWITHHELD and the run halts asking for a person, which is the closed answer and an available one.\nNO NEW MODULE EDGE - it reads posture through origin-posture.ts, which already reads the\nintegrations/ declaration through a STRUCTURAL type (the schedules/supervisor.ts pattern), so\nautomation/ still reaches integrations/ only through seams wired at the composition root.\n\nCORRECTION TO annot-automation-primitives-20260818 (above), WHICH THIS FALSIFIES. That note ends\n\"NO consumer wired yet - P4 (egress opt-in + bridge-only routing) and P3.3 (typist vs attended\nre-auth) consume it rather than re-deciding\". P4 IS THAT CONSUMER AND IT IS NOW WIRED:\norigin-posture.ts is read by locality.ts (where the step runs), by credential-gate.ts (whether the\nhosted typist may be reached at all, and whether a ceremony machine becomes a preference - it does\nfor adversarial origins only), and by session-establishment.ts (requiresAttendedAuth). The\nDISCOVERY_BUDGET half of that same note is still unconsumed; only the posture sentence is stale.\n\nNEW SEAM: EgressCandidateResolver (automation/seams.ts), bound at the composition root to\nbridge/registry.ts egressCandidatesForOrg. Direction is the usual one - automation/ never imports\nbridge/, and server.ts is the only place the two meet. DEFAULT DENY: the unbound resolver answers\n[], which REFUSES rather than widening egress, so a half-wired process cannot route a run anywhere\nnew. Org-scoped by construction (Rule 5): a machine in another tenant is not a candidate at all\nrather than one filtered later. Isolation suite: tests/security/locality-isolation.test.ts.\n\nNEW DEEP IMPORT: engine.ts now imports egress-policy.ts VALUES (residentialEgressPairings,\nmachineRetired), not only its types, and locality.ts's SESSION_MACHINE_RETIRED_REASON. Both are\ninside automation/, so no tier boundary moves.\n\nROUND FIVE, AND WHY IT IS A STRUCTURAL NOTE RATHER THAN A FIX LOG. The run loop never passed\nresidentialAvailable to the credential gate, so checkoutSession received [] and refused EVERY\nattended session - making the entire P4.2 ceremony-preference path unreachable in production. The\nfleet fact now flows engine.ts -> credential-gate.ts -> session-establishment.ts ->\ncofre/session-checkout.ts, and it is derived by the SAME predicate egress selection uses\n(residentialEgressPairings, egress-policy.ts). That shared predicate is the structural point: \"this\nmachine may carry the work\" and \"this machine's session may be released\" are two answers about one\nmachine, and two copies of the filter would let them drift apart silently, in the direction where a\nsession is unwrapped for a route that does not exist. machineRetired is the second shared predicate,\nasked by locality.ts (about a learned preference) and by engine.ts (about the machine checkout\nnamed), so a retired ceremony machine produces one halt whichever side reaches it first.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 locality, rounds 1-5: the module, the seam, the deep import, and the\ncorrection to the origin-posture note above).\n\nautomation/locality.ts - NEW tier-5 module inside automation/, imported by engine.ts. PURE by\nconstruction: no store, no seam, no env var, every input an argument - which is what makes its whole\ndecision table drivable from a test, including the cross-tenant cases. It folds the step's\ndeclaration, the origin's POSTURE (origin-posture.ts) and the fleet's answer (egress-policy.ts) into\none verdict - bridge / in-process / blocked - and answers a second question beside it:\nhostedTypistPermitFor, whether the hosted typist may type this step's password AND THROUGH WHICH\nDOOR. One rule: the login leaves by the same door as the work; when it cannot, the permit is\nWITHHELD and the run halts asking for a person, which is the closed answer and an available one.\nNO NEW MODULE EDGE - it reads posture through origin-posture.ts, which already reads the\nintegrations/ declaration through a STRUCTURAL type (the schedules/supervisor.ts pattern), so\nautomation/ still reaches integrations/ only through seams wired at the composition root.\n\nCORRECTION TO annot-automation-primitives-20260818 (above), WHICH THIS FALSIFIES. That note ends\n\"NO consumer wired yet - P4 (egress opt-in + bridge-only routing) and P3.3 (typist vs attended\nre-auth) consume it rather than re-deciding\". P4 IS THAT CONSUMER AND IT IS NOW WIRED:\norigin-posture.ts is read by locality.ts (where the step runs), by credential-gate.ts (whether the\nhosted typist may be reached at all, and whether a ceremony machine becomes a preference - it does\nfor adversarial origins only), and by session-establishment.ts (requiresAttendedAuth). The\nDISCOVERY_BUDGET half of that same note is still unconsumed; only the posture sentence is stale.\n\nNEW SEAM: EgressCandidateResolver (automation/seams.ts), bound at the composition root to\nbridge/registry.ts egressCandidatesForOrg. Direction is the usual one - automation/ never imports\nbridge/, and server.ts is the only place the two meet. DEFAULT DENY: the unbound resolver answers\n[], which REFUSES rather than widening egress, so a half-wired process cannot route a run anywhere\nnew. Org-scoped by construction (Rule 5): a machine in another tenant is not a candidate at all\nrather than one filtered later. Isolation suite: tests/security/locality-isolation.test.ts.\n\nNEW DEEP IMPORT: engine.ts now imports egress-policy.ts VALUES (residentialEgressPairings,\nmachineRetired), not only its types, and locality.ts's SESSION_MACHINE_RETIRED_REASON. Both are\ninside automation/, so no tier boundary moves.\n\nROUND FIVE, AND WHY IT IS A STRUCTURAL NOTE RATHER THAN A FIX LOG. The run loop never passed\nresidentialAvailable to the credential gate, so checkoutSession received [] and refused EVERY\nattended session - making the entire P4.2 ceremony-preference path unreachable in production. The\nfleet fact now flows engine.ts -> credential-gate.ts -> session-establishment.ts ->\ncofre/session-checkout.ts, and it is derived by the SAME predicate egress selection uses\n(residentialEgressPairings, egress-policy.ts). That shared predicate is the structural point: \"this\nmachine may carry the work\" and \"this machine's session may be released\" are two answers about one\nmachine, and two copies of the filter would let them drift apart silently, in the direction where a\nsession is unwrapped for a route that does not exist. machineRetired is the second shared predicate,\nasked by locality.ts (about a learned preference) and by engine.ts (about the machine checkout\nnamed), so a retired ceremony machine produces one halt whichever side reaches it first."
