      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND SEVEN: one module may build a refusal, and the run's live facts\nbecome arguments to it).\n\nMODULE-BOUNDARY CHANGE. `automation/locality.ts` gains a second entry point, `narrowLocalityForRun`,\nand with it two decisions that used to be made in `automation/engine.ts`:\n  - the POSTURE-DRIFT refusal - the hosted browser has navigated off the origin this step's posture\n    was declared for - previously built inline in `resolveLocalityForStep`;\n  - the ROUTE-SWITCH refusal - this step resolved to a different route out than the context this run\n    already launched - previously built inline in `refusalRecordFor`.\nBoth are PURE over three arguments the engine now GATHERS rather than judges: the URL the hosted\nbrowser is on, the origin the step declared, and the route the open context was launched for. The\nproperty that makes this module testable - no store, no seam, no env var, so the whole decision table\nis drivable from a test - is preserved by construction.\n\nWHY THIS IS A BOUNDARY CHANGE AND NOT A TIDY-UP. `LocalityVerdict`'s blocked member now carries a\nmodule-private `unique symbol` brand, so no module other than `locality.ts` can construct a refusal at\nall - enforced by the type system rather than by review. That is what makes the cross-product census in\ntests/automation/locality.test.ts a census of the PRODUCT rather than of one exported function: for six\nrounds a refusal built in engine.ts sat outside it, neutral against the failure ceiling, repeating\nidentically for ever.\n\nSEAM READS: unchanged in shape, memoised in cost. `loadIntegrationActionDeclaration` is reached through\na per-run memo keyed by (integrationKey, actionName) - `engine.ts` `loadDeclarationOnce` - and the same\nmemo is handed to the credential gate as a dep, so both `resolveStepOrigin` walks share one read per\nrow. KEYED BY THE LOOKUP, NEVER BY THE STEP: a per-run map holding a value resolved for one step and\nreused for another is exactly the shape that produced the run-level `preferredPairingId` defect in\nround four.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND SEVEN: one module may build a refusal, and the run's live facts\nbecome arguments to it).\n\nMODULE-BOUNDARY CHANGE. `automation/locality.ts` gains a second entry point, `narrowLocalityForRun`,\nand with it two decisions that used to be made in `automation/engine.ts`:\n  - the POSTURE-DRIFT refusal - the hosted browser has navigated off the origin this step's posture\n    was declared for - previously built inline in `resolveLocalityForStep`;\n  - the ROUTE-SWITCH refusal - this step resolved to a different route out than the context this run\n    already launched - previously built inline in `refusalRecordFor`.\nBoth are PURE over three arguments the engine now GATHERS rather than judges: the URL the hosted\nbrowser is on, the origin the step declared, and the route the open context was launched for. The\nproperty that makes this module testable - no store, no seam, no env var, so the whole decision table\nis drivable from a test - is preserved by construction.\n\nWHY THIS IS A BOUNDARY CHANGE AND NOT A TIDY-UP. `LocalityVerdict`'s blocked member now carries a\nmodule-private `unique symbol` brand, so no module other than `locality.ts` can construct a refusal at\nall - enforced by the type system rather than by review. That is what makes the cross-product census in\ntests/automation/locality.test.ts a census of the PRODUCT rather than of one exported function: for six\nrounds a refusal built in engine.ts sat outside it, neutral against the failure ceiling, repeating\nidentically for ever.\n\nSEAM READS: unchanged in shape, memoised in cost. `loadIntegrationActionDeclaration` is reached through\na per-run memo keyed by (integrationKey, actionName) - `engine.ts` `loadDeclarationOnce` - and the same\nmemo is handed to the credential gate as a dep, so both `resolveStepOrigin` walks share one read per\nrow. KEYED BY THE LOOKUP, NEVER BY THE STEP: a per-run map holding a value resolved for one step and\nreused for another is exactly the shape that produced the run-level `preferredPairingId` defect in\nround four.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND SEVEN: one module may build a refusal, and the run's live facts\nbecome arguments to it).\n\nMODULE-BOUNDARY CHANGE. `automation/locality.ts` gains a second entry point, `narrowLocalityForRun`,\nand with it two decisions that used to be made in `automation/engine.ts`:\n  - the POSTURE-DRIFT refusal - the hosted browser has navigated off the origin this step's posture\n    was declared for - previously built inline in `resolveLocalityForStep`;\n  - the ROUTE-SWITCH refusal - this step resolved to a different route out than the context this run\n    already launched - previously built inline in `refusalRecordFor`.\nBoth are PURE over three arguments the engine now GATHERS rather than judges: the URL the hosted\nbrowser is on, the origin the step declared, and the route the open context was launched for. The\nproperty that makes this module testable - no store, no seam, no env var, so the whole decision table\nis drivable from a test - is preserved by construction.\n\nWHY THIS IS A BOUNDARY CHANGE AND NOT A TIDY-UP. `LocalityVerdict`'s blocked member now carries a\nmodule-private `unique symbol` brand, so no module other than `locality.ts` can construct a refusal at\nall - enforced by the type system rather than by review. That is what makes the cross-product census in\ntests/automation/locality.test.ts a census of the PRODUCT rather than of one exported function: for six\nrounds a refusal built in engine.ts sat outside it, neutral against the failure ceiling, repeating\nidentically for ever.\n\nSEAM READS: unchanged in shape, memoised in cost. `loadIntegrationActionDeclaration` is reached through\na per-run memo keyed by (integrationKey, actionName) - `engine.ts` `loadDeclarationOnce` - and the same\nmemo is handed to the credential gate as a dep, so both `resolveStepOrigin` walks share one read per\nrow. KEYED BY THE LOOKUP, NEVER BY THE STEP: a per-run map holding a value resolved for one step and\nreused for another is exactly the shape that produced the run-level `preferredPairingId` defect in\nround four."
    },
