      "type": "text",
      "id": "annot-p4-egress-endpoint-module-20260819",
      "containerId": null,
      "x": 60,
      "y": 20872.5,
      "width": 900.0,
      "height": 385.0,
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 3,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "strokeColor": "#b91c1c",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "seed": 202608203,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 202608204,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1755615600000,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND EIGHT: one new file in `bridge/`, no new module boundary).\n\napi/src/bridge/egress-endpoint.ts (NEW)\n  The validator for a machine's SELF-ASSERTED egress address. A pure function with NO imports -\n  no store, no config module, no seam - so it adds no edge to the tier graph and cannot be a\n  cycle. It sits in `bridge/` because that is where the value ENTERS the system (the `hello`\n  frame) and where its only two writers live.\n    normaliseEgressEndpoint(raw, policy) -> canonical `scheme://host[:port]` | null\n    egressEndpointPolicy()               -> reads EKOA_BRIDGE_ALLOW_PRIVATE_EGRESS (closed default)\n  Two callers inside the module, and deliberately both:\n    bridge/server.ts     the `hello` handler - refuses at INGRESS and warns the operator\n    bridge/registry.ts   `registerPairing` (the write) and `egressCandidatesForOrg` (the read that\n                         selection consumes), so a row written by any other path is re-judged\n                         before it can become a launch option\n  `automation/` still does not import `bridge/` and does not need to: the candidate list it\n  receives through `setEgressCandidateResolver` already carries only authorised addresses, so the\n  one-way tier rule is unchanged by this fix.\n\napi/src/bridge/capability-grants.ts\n  CapabilityGrantDoc gains `egressEndpoint` - the address an `egress.residential` grant authorises.\n  Same module, same store, additive field; `grantCapability` throws CapabilityGrantError when that\n  capability is granted without a usable one.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND EIGHT: one new file in `bridge/`, no new module boundary).\n\napi/src/bridge/egress-endpoint.ts (NEW)\n  The validator for a machine's SELF-ASSERTED egress address. A pure function with NO imports -\n  no store, no config module, no seam - so it adds no edge to the tier graph and cannot be a\n  cycle. It sits in `bridge/` because that is where the value ENTERS the system (the `hello`\n  frame) and where its only two writers live.\n    normaliseEgressEndpoint(raw, policy) -> canonical `scheme://host[:port]` | null\n    egressEndpointPolicy()               -> reads EKOA_BRIDGE_ALLOW_PRIVATE_EGRESS (closed default)\n  Two callers inside the module, and deliberately both:\n    bridge/server.ts     the `hello` handler - refuses at INGRESS and warns the operator\n    bridge/registry.ts   `registerPairing` (the write) and `egressCandidatesForOrg` (the read that\n                         selection consumes), so a row written by any other path is re-judged\n                         before it can become a launch option\n  `automation/` still does not import `bridge/` and does not need to: the candidate list it\n  receives through `setEgressCandidateResolver` already carries only authorised addresses, so the\n  one-way tier rule is unchanged by this fix.\n\napi/src/bridge/capability-grants.ts\n  CapabilityGrantDoc gains `egressEndpoint` - the address an `egress.residential` grant authorises.\n  Same module, same store, additive field; `grantCapability` throws CapabilityGrantError when that\n  capability is granted without a usable one.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND EIGHT: one new file in `bridge/`, no new module boundary).\n\napi/src/bridge/egress-endpoint.ts (NEW)\n  The validator for a machine's SELF-ASSERTED egress address. A pure function with NO imports -\n  no store, no config module, no seam - so it adds no edge to the tier graph and cannot be a\n  cycle. It sits in `bridge/` because that is where the value ENTERS the system (the `hello`\n  frame) and where its only two writers live.\n    normaliseEgressEndpoint(raw, policy) -> canonical `scheme://host[:port]` | null\n    egressEndpointPolicy()               -> reads EKOA_BRIDGE_ALLOW_PRIVATE_EGRESS (closed default)\n  Two callers inside the module, and deliberately both:\n    bridge/server.ts     the `hello` handler - refuses at INGRESS and warns the operator\n    bridge/registry.ts   `registerPairing` (the write) and `egressCandidatesForOrg` (the read that\n                         selection consumes), so a row written by any other path is re-judged\n                         before it can become a launch option\n  `automation/` still does not import `bridge/` and does not need to: the candidate list it\n  receives through `setEgressCandidateResolver` already carries only authorised addresses, so the\n  one-way tier rule is unchanged by this fix.\n\napi/src/bridge/capability-grants.ts\n  CapabilityGrantDoc gains `egressEndpoint` - the address an `egress.residential` grant authorises.\n  Same module, same store, additive field; `grantCapability` throws CapabilityGrantError when that\n  capability is granted without a usable one."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "annot-p4-composition-root-seam-20260819",
      "containerId": null,
      "x": 60,
      "y": 21337.5,
      "width": 900.0,
      "height": 385.0,
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 3,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "strokeColor": "#0e7490",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "seed": 202608205,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 202608206,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1755615600000,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND EIGHT: the composition root gains ONE optional runtime dep, so its\nown bindings can be observed).\n\napi/src/server.ts\n  RuntimeDeps { now, genId, openBrowser? }\n    `openBrowser` defaults to `services/browser-pool.ts` `getSharedBrowser`, so production and\n    every existing `buildApp(cfg, {now, genId})` caller are unchanged. It exists because the ONE\n    line that turns a resolved egress route into an actually-proxied context -\n    `setLocalBrowserContextProvider(localBrowserContextProviderUsing(...))` - reached a real\n    Playwright launch, so observing it meant launching Chromium, so nothing observed it and\n    deleting the line left the whole suite green.\n  No new module and no new edge: `server.ts` already imports `services/browser-pool.ts`, and the\n  dep only chooses WHICH opener that same binding is handed.\n\napi/tests/automation/composition-root-locality.test.ts (NEW)\n  Boots the REAL buildApp after `__resetAutomationSeamsForTests()` and asserts the two P4 seams\n  answer as the real implementations rather than as \"something is installed\":\n    loadEgressCandidates(org) -> `[]` for an org with no machines (the statement the unbound\n      `null` default CANNOT make), the org's own rows with the advertised-and-granted\n      intersection, and nothing from another tenant.\n    getLocalBrowserContext(user, egress) -> newContext({proxy:{server}}) for a machine route and\n      a bare newContext({}) for the datacenter route.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND EIGHT: the composition root gains ONE optional runtime dep, so its\nown bindings can be observed).\n\napi/src/server.ts\n  RuntimeDeps { now, genId, openBrowser? }\n    `openBrowser` defaults to `services/browser-pool.ts` `getSharedBrowser`, so production and\n    every existing `buildApp(cfg, {now, genId})` caller are unchanged. It exists because the ONE\n    line that turns a resolved egress route into an actually-proxied context -\n    `setLocalBrowserContextProvider(localBrowserContextProviderUsing(...))` - reached a real\n    Playwright launch, so observing it meant launching Chromium, so nothing observed it and\n    deleting the line left the whole suite green.\n  No new module and no new edge: `server.ts` already imports `services/browser-pool.ts`, and the\n  dep only chooses WHICH opener that same binding is handed.\n\napi/tests/automation/composition-root-locality.test.ts (NEW)\n  Boots the REAL buildApp after `__resetAutomationSeamsForTests()` and asserts the two P4 seams\n  answer as the real implementations rather than as \"something is installed\":\n    loadEgressCandidates(org) -> `[]` for an org with no machines (the statement the unbound\n      `null` default CANNOT make), the org's own rows with the advertised-and-granted\n      intersection, and nothing from another tenant.\n    getLocalBrowserContext(user, egress) -> newContext({proxy:{server}}) for a machine route and\n      a bare newContext({}) for the datacenter route.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND EIGHT: the composition root gains ONE optional runtime dep, so its\nown bindings can be observed).\n\napi/src/server.ts\n  RuntimeDeps { now, genId, openBrowser? }\n    `openBrowser` defaults to `services/browser-pool.ts` `getSharedBrowser`, so production and\n    every existing `buildApp(cfg, {now, genId})` caller are unchanged. It exists because the ONE\n    line that turns a resolved egress route into an actually-proxied context -\n    `setLocalBrowserContextProvider(localBrowserContextProviderUsing(...))` - reached a real\n    Playwright launch, so observing it meant launching Chromium, so nothing observed it and\n    deleting the line left the whole suite green.\n  No new module and no new edge: `server.ts` already imports `services/browser-pool.ts`, and the\n  dep only chooses WHICH opener that same binding is handed.\n\napi/tests/automation/composition-root-locality.test.ts (NEW)\n  Boots the REAL buildApp after `__resetAutomationSeamsForTests()` and asserts the two P4 seams\n  answer as the real implementations rather than as \"something is installed\":\n    loadEgressCandidates(org) -> `[]` for an org with no machines (the statement the unbound\n      `null` default CANNOT make), the org's own rows with the advertised-and-granted\n      intersection, and nothing from another tenant.\n    getLocalBrowserContext(user, egress) -> newContext({proxy:{server}}) for a machine route and\n      a bare newContext({}) for the datacenter route."
    },
