    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "asbuilt-20260821g-s1r9-retention-gains-a-trigger",
      "x": 60,
      "y": 31490.0,
      "width": 747,
      "height": 595.0,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-21 (g) - S1 ROUND NINE: retention gains a TRIGGER. AMENDS (e) and (f).\n\nWHAT (e) LEFT WRONG, AND IT IS THE POINT OF THIS NOTE. (e) recorded that retention stops being\nanswered synchronously and that \"TTL is the collector\". TTL had NO TRIGGER. Between them,\nsweepExpiredEvidence and sweepExpiredScreenshots had exactly ONE caller chain in the estate:\n\n  server.ts sweepScreenshotsSparingPinnedEvidence -> bootState -> boot()\n\nNo setInterval, no Mongo TTL index anywhere in the repo - in a process that already runs THREE\ninterval rails (events/listener-supervisor, schedules/supervisor, knowledge/crawl/scheduler).\ndeploy/staging/docker-compose.yml is `restart: unless-stopped`; deploy/api.service.json is a\nlong-lived container over a persistent volume. Six months without a deploy = six months of every\ntenant's evidence rows AND, through their pins, six months of authenticated client-portal PNGs.\n\n1. THE NEW LIFECYCLE TRIGGER (no new module, no new import edge).\n   server.ts startRetentionSweepRail -> [unref'd setInterval, RETENTION_SWEEP_INTERVAL_MS = 6h]\n     -> server.ts retentionSweepTick -> sweepScreenshotsSparingPinnedEvidence (the SAME composition\n        bootState already awaited: evidence sweep -> pin read -> screenshot sweep, in that order)\n   Armed by bootState right after the one-shot; disarmed by the SIGINT/SIGTERM handler, which awaits\n   the pass in flight. Re-entrancy-guarded: a tick landing on an in-flight pass answers null.\n\n2. ARMED FROM bootState AND NOT FROM boot()'s POST-LISTEN BLOCK, unlike the other three rails.\n   Two reasons: no HTTP-listener dependency (delivery and the listener supervisor wait because\n   re-entrant calls need a live socket), and boot() is entered by NO test in this repo - the exact\n   defect class this slice already hit when the pin argument lived in an unentered bootState.\n\n3. NOT A MONGO TTL INDEX. It would collect the row and leave the SCREENSHOTS (a filesystem walk in\n   this process, needing a trigger regardless); it would take the evidence-before-pins ordering out\n   of this process's hands; and validatedAt is an ISO-8601 STRING by design, so an index would mean\n   changing the stored type or carrying a permanent parallel field (rule 10 forbids the second).\n\nUNCHANGED by this round: every module boundary, every import edge, every seam. The rail is a timer\nover a composition that already existed. What changed is that the composition now runs more than\nonce per deploy - which is what makes (e)'s \"TTL is the collector\" a true sentence.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-21 (g) - S1 ROUND NINE: retention gains a TRIGGER. AMENDS (e) and (f).\n\nWHAT (e) LEFT WRONG, AND IT IS THE POINT OF THIS NOTE. (e) recorded that retention stops being\nanswered synchronously and that \"TTL is the collector\". TTL had NO TRIGGER. Between them,\nsweepExpiredEvidence and sweepExpiredScreenshots had exactly ONE caller chain in the estate:\n\n  server.ts sweepScreenshotsSparingPinnedEvidence -> bootState -> boot()\n\nNo setInterval, no Mongo TTL index anywhere in the repo - in a process that already runs THREE\ninterval rails (events/listener-supervisor, schedules/supervisor, knowledge/crawl/scheduler).\ndeploy/staging/docker-compose.yml is `restart: unless-stopped`; deploy/api.service.json is a\nlong-lived container over a persistent volume. Six months without a deploy = six months of every\ntenant's evidence rows AND, through their pins, six months of authenticated client-portal PNGs.\n\n1. THE NEW LIFECYCLE TRIGGER (no new module, no new import edge).\n   server.ts startRetentionSweepRail -> [unref'd setInterval, RETENTION_SWEEP_INTERVAL_MS = 6h]\n     -> server.ts retentionSweepTick -> sweepScreenshotsSparingPinnedEvidence (the SAME composition\n        bootState already awaited: evidence sweep -> pin read -> screenshot sweep, in that order)\n   Armed by bootState right after the one-shot; disarmed by the SIGINT/SIGTERM handler, which awaits\n   the pass in flight. Re-entrancy-guarded: a tick landing on an in-flight pass answers null.\n\n2. ARMED FROM bootState AND NOT FROM boot()'s POST-LISTEN BLOCK, unlike the other three rails.\n   Two reasons: no HTTP-listener dependency (delivery and the listener supervisor wait because\n   re-entrant calls need a live socket), and boot() is entered by NO test in this repo - the exact\n   defect class this slice already hit when the pin argument lived in an unentered bootState.\n\n3. NOT A MONGO TTL INDEX. It would collect the row and leave the SCREENSHOTS (a filesystem walk in\n   this process, needing a trigger regardless); it would take the evidence-before-pins ordering out\n   of this process's hands; and validatedAt is an ISO-8601 STRING by design, so an index would mean\n   changing the stored type or carrying a permanent parallel field (rule 10 forbids the second).\n\nUNCHANGED by this round: every module boundary, every import edge, every seam. The rail is a timer\nover a composition that already existed. What changed is that the composition now runs more than\nonce per deploy - which is what makes (e)'s \"TTL is the collector\" a true sentence.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-21 (g) - S1 ROUND NINE: retention gains a TRIGGER. AMENDS (e) and (f).\n\nWHAT (e) LEFT WRONG, AND IT IS THE POINT OF THIS NOTE. (e) recorded that retention stops being\nanswered synchronously and that \"TTL is the collector\". TTL had NO TRIGGER. Between them,\nsweepExpiredEvidence and sweepExpiredScreenshots had exactly ONE caller chain in the estate:\n\n  server.ts sweepScreenshotsSparingPinnedEvidence -> bootState -> boot()\n\nNo setInterval, no Mongo TTL index anywhere in the repo - in a process that already runs THREE\ninterval rails (events/listener-supervisor, schedules/supervisor, knowledge/crawl/scheduler).\ndeploy/staging/docker-compose.yml is `restart: unless-stopped`; deploy/api.service.json is a\nlong-lived container over a persistent volume. Six months without a deploy = six months of every\ntenant's evidence rows AND, through their pins, six months of authenticated client-portal PNGs.\n\n1. THE NEW LIFECYCLE TRIGGER (no new module, no new import edge).\n   server.ts startRetentionSweepRail -> [unref'd setInterval, RETENTION_SWEEP_INTERVAL_MS = 6h]\n     -> server.ts retentionSweepTick -> sweepScreenshotsSparingPinnedEvidence (the SAME composition\n        bootState already awaited: evidence sweep -> pin read -> screenshot sweep, in that order)\n   Armed by bootState right after the one-shot; disarmed by the SIGINT/SIGTERM handler, which awaits\n   the pass in flight. Re-entrancy-guarded: a tick landing on an in-flight pass answers null.\n\n2. ARMED FROM bootState AND NOT FROM boot()'s POST-LISTEN BLOCK, unlike the other three rails.\n   Two reasons: no HTTP-listener dependency (delivery and the listener supervisor wait because\n   re-entrant calls need a live socket), and boot() is entered by NO test in this repo - the exact\n   defect class this slice already hit when the pin argument lived in an unentered bootState.\n\n3. NOT A MONGO TTL INDEX. It would collect the row and leave the SCREENSHOTS (a filesystem walk in\n   this process, needing a trigger regardless); it would take the evidence-before-pins ordering out\n   of this process's hands; and validatedAt is an ISO-8601 STRING by design, so an index would mean\n   changing the stored type or carrying a permanent parallel field (rule 10 forbids the second).\n\nUNCHANGED by this round: every module boundary, every import edge, every seam. The rail is a timer\nover a composition that already existed. What changed is that the composition now runs more than\nonce per deploy - which is what makes (e)'s \"TTL is the collector\" a true sentence.",
