      "updated": 1755640000000,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-20 (S4+S5) - THE REUSE LADDER: `achieve` grows two rungs between reuse and mint.\nTWO NEW TIER-3 MODULES, ONE NEW TIER-2 MODULE, TWO NEW SEAMS, ONE NEW RUNTIME EDGE. The PICK is unchanged.\n\nWHAT DID NOT MOVE, AND IT IS THE WHOLE SAFETY ARGUMENT\n  matchActionForGoal is UNTOUCHED. It is still the only producer of a match, still lexical, still\n  coverage-not-overlap, and neither new rung is handed `definition.actions`. No model picks an action.\n  The gate is still the executor's: integration-achieve.ts makes exactly ONE call to\n  executeIntegrationCapabilityAction, whatever the rungs above decided, and neither new module can\n  execute, approve or store anything at all (pinned statically in achieve-reuse-ladder.test.ts).\n\nNEW  api/src/data/simple-query.ts (tier 2)   - THE one single-field row predicate.\n  evalQuery's nine comparison semantics lifted OUT of automation/platform-primitives.ts so the compose\n  rung can share them: integrations/ (tier 3) may not import automation/ (tier 5), and a second copy of\n  nine comparisons is a drift waiting to happen. Semantics carried VERBATIM, coercion edges included.\n  automation/platform-primitives.ts keeps only the recipe-only half (resolving a {{captured.x}} ref\n  into the compared value) and re-exports the SimpleQuery type, so no shipped recipe changes meaning.\n  NEW EDGE: integrations/action-compose.ts -> data/simple-query.ts (tier 3 -> tier 2, downward).\n\nNEW  api/src/integrations/action-parametrize.ts (tier 3) - the PARAMETRIZE rung's locked guardrails,\n  written as authored-action.ts is written: pure functions, a named check list, a verdict not a throw.\n    declared_args  - THE ONLY CHECK argsSchema EVER GETS. The executor never reads it (grep api/src:\n                     the field appears in the type, in achieve's prompt, in action-consent's EXCLUDED\n                     list and in a catalog summary, and nowhere else), while buildVars merges every key\n                     of args into the one {{name}} namespace the templates interpolate from. Also\n                     refuses a key naming a configSchema field, and a key the caller already supplied.\n    targeting      - DECISION D1. An argument landing in the BODY is covered by the standing\n                     shape+destination approval exactly as a caller-supplied one is; an argument\n                     landing in the resolved TARGET selects the RESOURCE under an approval whose dialog\n                     only ever showed {{arg}}. So: body always, target only on a literal mutates:false.\n                     Headers count as target (the dialog shows method + resolved URL, never a header).\n                     The rung applies D1 BEFORE the model call too: a targeting argument on a write is\n                     never offered, so such a call behaves exactly as it does today rather than newly\n                     refusing.\n    no_pasted_secret / render - authored-action.ts's checks 7 and 8, applied to values. The render probe\n                     is DEFENCE IN DEPTH and recorded as such: action-executor.ts already asserts the\n                     resolved URL's origin at call time. What this adds is WHEN - a coded refusal the\n                     caller can act on, before the write gate is consulted and before any credential is\n                     decrypted, instead of discovering an unsendable plan by trying to send it.\n\nNEW  api/src/integrations/action-compose.ts (tier 3) - the COMPOSE rung's stage and its guardrails.\n  Compose is NOT a prompt slice: there is no server-side join in this repo. CollectionsEngine is\n  list/get/create/importCreate/upsert/delete, and store.query is list + an in-memory single-field\n  filter. So the stage IS the addition: run the matched trusted READ, then filter one tenant collection\n  with a SimpleQuery-class predicate and join on one field of each side. The model contributes three\n  strings and a value; every row that moves is moved by TypeScript.\n  read_only is checked BEFORE anything executes (the stage's first move is to RUN the action), so a\n  composed write is refused rather than performed and then discussed.\n\nTWO NEW SEAMS on AchieveContext, both bound ONCE in server.ts, both ABSENT-MEANS-SKIP:\n  planStep : PlanDrafter   - the THIRD authorWithRepair specialisation, beside ActionDrafter and the\n                             automation planner's. ONE seam for BOTH rungs: they differ only in the\n                             output contract and the parser, which the core already takes as arguments.\n                             `draft` is `unknown`; each rung's deterministic suite is what types it.\n  appCollections : AppCollections - list/read over the tenant's app_data. The binding is where tenancy\n                             is decided, and it uses the platform's OWN predicate (listArtifacts(actor)\n                             -> OwnerVisibilityScoped.listVisible) rather than an orgId comparison\n                             written at the root, then sharedScope(artifactId, ownerUserId) exactly as\n                             the setAppDataStore binding does. AMBIGUITY IS AN ANSWER: a collection name\n                             two of the org's artifacts both hold refuses instead of picking one.\n  Absent either seam, achieve behaves precisely as it did before this slice - the Rule-7 promise, and\n  what makes the rungs safe to add to a live capability endpoint. Both bindings are pinned by\n  tests/contract/integrations-reuse-ladder.test.ts, which was verified to go red with either deleted.\n\ndata/collections-engine.ts gains ONE read: listCollections(scope) - distinct names, never a row, on the\n  same single query-binding point every other read uses. It exists so the compose prompt can NAME the\n  tenant's collections without listing every row of each to discover them.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-20 (S4+S5) - THE REUSE LADDER: `achieve` grows two rungs between reuse and mint.\nTWO NEW TIER-3 MODULES, ONE NEW TIER-2 MODULE, TWO NEW SEAMS, ONE NEW RUNTIME EDGE. The PICK is unchanged.\n\nWHAT DID NOT MOVE, AND IT IS THE WHOLE SAFETY ARGUMENT\n  matchActionForGoal is UNTOUCHED. It is still the only producer of a match, still lexical, still\n  coverage-not-overlap, and neither new rung is handed `definition.actions`. No model picks an action.\n  The gate is still the executor's: integration-achieve.ts makes exactly ONE call to\n  executeIntegrationCapabilityAction, whatever the rungs above decided, and neither new module can\n  execute, approve or store anything at all (pinned statically in achieve-reuse-ladder.test.ts).\n\nNEW  api/src/data/simple-query.ts (tier 2)   - THE one single-field row predicate.\n  evalQuery's nine comparison semantics lifted OUT of automation/platform-primitives.ts so the compose\n  rung can share them: integrations/ (tier 3) may not import automation/ (tier 5), and a second copy of\n  nine comparisons is a drift waiting to happen. Semantics carried VERBATIM, coercion edges included.\n  automation/platform-primitives.ts keeps only the recipe-only half (resolving a {{captured.x}} ref\n  into the compared value) and re-exports the SimpleQuery type, so no shipped recipe changes meaning.\n  NEW EDGE: integrations/action-compose.ts -> data/simple-query.ts (tier 3 -> tier 2, downward).\n\nNEW  api/src/integrations/action-parametrize.ts (tier 3) - the PARAMETRIZE rung's locked guardrails,\n  written as authored-action.ts is written: pure functions, a named check list, a verdict not a throw.\n    declared_args  - THE ONLY CHECK argsSchema EVER GETS. The executor never reads it (grep api/src:\n                     the field appears in the type, in achieve's prompt, in action-consent's EXCLUDED\n                     list and in a catalog summary, and nowhere else), while buildVars merges every key\n                     of args into the one {{name}} namespace the templates interpolate from. Also\n                     refuses a key naming a configSchema field, and a key the caller already supplied.\n    targeting      - DECISION D1. An argument landing in the BODY is covered by the standing\n                     shape+destination approval exactly as a caller-supplied one is; an argument\n                     landing in the resolved TARGET selects the RESOURCE under an approval whose dialog\n                     only ever showed {{arg}}. So: body always, target only on a literal mutates:false.\n                     Headers count as target (the dialog shows method + resolved URL, never a header).\n                     The rung applies D1 BEFORE the model call too: a targeting argument on a write is\n                     never offered, so such a call behaves exactly as it does today rather than newly\n                     refusing.\n    no_pasted_secret / render - authored-action.ts's checks 7 and 8, applied to values. The render probe\n                     is DEFENCE IN DEPTH and recorded as such: action-executor.ts already asserts the\n                     resolved URL's origin at call time. What this adds is WHEN - a coded refusal the\n                     caller can act on, before the write gate is consulted and before any credential is\n                     decrypted, instead of discovering an unsendable plan by trying to send it.\n\nNEW  api/src/integrations/action-compose.ts (tier 3) - the COMPOSE rung's stage and its guardrails.\n  Compose is NOT a prompt slice: there is no server-side join in this repo. CollectionsEngine is\n  list/get/create/importCreate/upsert/delete, and store.query is list + an in-memory single-field\n  filter. So the stage IS the addition: run the matched trusted READ, then filter one tenant collection\n  with a SimpleQuery-class predicate and join on one field of each side. The model contributes three\n  strings and a value; every row that moves is moved by TypeScript.\n  read_only is checked BEFORE anything executes (the stage's first move is to RUN the action), so a\n  composed write is refused rather than performed and then discussed.\n\nTWO NEW SEAMS on AchieveContext, both bound ONCE in server.ts, both ABSENT-MEANS-SKIP:\n  planStep : PlanDrafter   - the THIRD authorWithRepair specialisation, beside ActionDrafter and the\n                             automation planner's. ONE seam for BOTH rungs: they differ only in the\n                             output contract and the parser, which the core already takes as arguments.\n                             `draft` is `unknown`; each rung's deterministic suite is what types it.\n  appCollections : AppCollections - list/read over the tenant's app_data. The binding is where tenancy\n                             is decided, and it uses the platform's OWN predicate (listArtifacts(actor)\n                             -> OwnerVisibilityScoped.listVisible) rather than an orgId comparison\n                             written at the root, then sharedScope(artifactId, ownerUserId) exactly as\n                             the setAppDataStore binding does. AMBIGUITY IS AN ANSWER: a collection name\n                             two of the org's artifacts both hold refuses instead of picking one.\n  Absent either seam, achieve behaves precisely as it did before this slice - the Rule-7 promise, and\n  what makes the rungs safe to add to a live capability endpoint. Both bindings are pinned by\n  tests/contract/integrations-reuse-ladder.test.ts, which was verified to go red with either deleted.\n\ndata/collections-engine.ts gains ONE read: listCollections(scope) - distinct names, never a row, on the\n  same single query-binding point every other read uses. It exists so the compose prompt can NAME the\n  tenant's collections without listing every row of each to discover them.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-20 (S4+S5) - THE REUSE LADDER: `achieve` grows two rungs between reuse and mint.\nTWO NEW TIER-3 MODULES, ONE NEW TIER-2 MODULE, TWO NEW SEAMS, ONE NEW RUNTIME EDGE. The PICK is unchanged.\n\nWHAT DID NOT MOVE, AND IT IS THE WHOLE SAFETY ARGUMENT\n  matchActionForGoal is UNTOUCHED. It is still the only producer of a match, still lexical, still\n  coverage-not-overlap, and neither new rung is handed `definition.actions`. No model picks an action.\n  The gate is still the executor's: integration-achieve.ts makes exactly ONE call to\n  executeIntegrationCapabilityAction, whatever the rungs above decided, and neither new module can\n  execute, approve or store anything at all (pinned statically in achieve-reuse-ladder.test.ts).\n\nNEW  api/src/data/simple-query.ts (tier 2)   - THE one single-field row predicate.\n  evalQuery's nine comparison semantics lifted OUT of automation/platform-primitives.ts so the compose\n  rung can share them: integrations/ (tier 3) may not import automation/ (tier 5), and a second copy of\n  nine comparisons is a drift waiting to happen. Semantics carried VERBATIM, coercion edges included.\n  automation/platform-primitives.ts keeps only the recipe-only half (resolving a {{captured.x}} ref\n  into the compared value) and re-exports the SimpleQuery type, so no shipped recipe changes meaning.\n  NEW EDGE: integrations/action-compose.ts -> data/simple-query.ts (tier 3 -> tier 2, downward).\n\nNEW  api/src/integrations/action-parametrize.ts (tier 3) - the PARAMETRIZE rung's locked guardrails,\n  written as authored-action.ts is written: pure functions, a named check list, a verdict not a throw.\n    declared_args  - THE ONLY CHECK argsSchema EVER GETS. The executor never reads it (grep api/src:\n                     the field appears in the type, in achieve's prompt, in action-consent's EXCLUDED\n                     list and in a catalog summary, and nowhere else), while buildVars merges every key\n                     of args into the one {{name}} namespace the templates interpolate from. Also\n                     refuses a key naming a configSchema field, and a key the caller already supplied.\n    targeting      - DECISION D1. An argument landing in the BODY is covered by the standing\n                     shape+destination approval exactly as a caller-supplied one is; an argument\n                     landing in the resolved TARGET selects the RESOURCE under an approval whose dialog\n                     only ever showed {{arg}}. So: body always, target only on a literal mutates:false.\n                     Headers count as target (the dialog shows method + resolved URL, never a header).\n                     The rung applies D1 BEFORE the model call too: a targeting argument on a write is\n                     never offered, so such a call behaves exactly as it does today rather than newly\n                     refusing.\n    no_pasted_secret / render - authored-action.ts's checks 7 and 8, applied to values. The render probe\n                     is DEFENCE IN DEPTH and recorded as such: action-executor.ts already asserts the\n                     resolved URL's origin at call time. What this adds is WHEN - a coded refusal the\n                     caller can act on, before the write gate is consulted and before any credential is\n                     decrypted, instead of discovering an unsendable plan by trying to send it.\n\nNEW  api/src/integrations/action-compose.ts (tier 3) - the COMPOSE rung's stage and its guardrails.\n  Compose is NOT a prompt slice: there is no server-side join in this repo. CollectionsEngine is\n  list/get/create/importCreate/upsert/delete, and store.query is list + an in-memory single-field\n  filter. So the stage IS the addition: run the matched trusted READ, then filter one tenant collection\n  with a SimpleQuery-class predicate and join on one field of each side. The model contributes three\n  strings and a value; every row that moves is moved by TypeScript.\n  read_only is checked BEFORE anything executes (the stage's first move is to RUN the action), so a\n  composed write is refused rather than performed and then discussed.\n\nTWO NEW SEAMS on AchieveContext, both bound ONCE in server.ts, both ABSENT-MEANS-SKIP:\n  planStep : PlanDrafter   - the THIRD authorWithRepair specialisation, beside ActionDrafter and the\n                             automation planner's. ONE seam for BOTH rungs: they differ only in the\n                             output contract and the parser, which the core already takes as arguments.\n                             `draft` is `unknown`; each rung's deterministic suite is what types it.\n  appCollections : AppCollections - list/read over the tenant's app_data. The binding is where tenancy\n                             is decided, and it uses the platform's OWN predicate (listArtifacts(actor)\n                             -> OwnerVisibilityScoped.listVisible) rather than an orgId comparison\n                             written at the root, then sharedScope(artifactId, ownerUserId) exactly as\n                             the setAppDataStore binding does. AMBIGUITY IS AN ANSWER: a collection name\n                             two of the org's artifacts both hold refuses instead of picking one.\n  Absent either seam, achieve behaves precisely as it did before this slice - the Rule-7 promise, and\n  what makes the rungs safe to add to a live capability endpoint. Both bindings are pinned by\n  tests/contract/integrations-reuse-ladder.test.ts, which was verified to go red with either deleted.\n\ndata/collections-engine.ts gains ONE read: listCollections(scope) - distinct names, never a row, on the\n  same single query-binding point every other read uses. It exists so the compose prompt can NAME the\n  tenant's collections without listing every row of each to discover them."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "correction-20260820b-s5-owner-scope",
      "containerId": null,
      "x": 60,
      "y": 27615.0,
      "width": 1060,
      "height": 1032.5,
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 3,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "strokeColor": "#b45309",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "seed": 202608211,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 202608212,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1755740000000,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "text": "CORRECTION 2026-08-20b (S5 verification round two) - THE COMPOSE RUNG'S UNIT IS THE OWNER.\nSupersedes four claims in the AS-BUILT note above. Decision journal: D-S5-1. No new module, no new\ncollection, no new scope key: the same `usr.<owner>` key that already existed, bound correctly.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG, AND IT WAS ONE MISTAKE FOUR TIMES: a decision scoped to a unit the thing decided\nabout does not have.\n\n  data/collections-engine.ts - THE FACT THAT SETTLES IT. `sharedScope(appId, ownerUserId)` returns\n  `{ scopeKey: 'usr.<ownerUserId>', appId }`, and `Scope.appId` IS NEVER PART OF ANY QUERY: `docId`\n  and all six reads bind on `scope.scopeKey` alone. Shared `app_data` therefore has NO artifact\n  dimension, and \"the collections of app X\" is not a question this store can answer.\n  NEW: `ownerSharedScope(ownerUserId)` states the per-owner unit in the type, so a caller stops\n  reconstructing it from an `appId` the store ignores. `sharedScope` stays for the serving planes,\n  which genuinely hold an app and pass it through.\n\n  server.ts, the `appCollections` binding - REPLACED, and this is the tenancy decision (Rule 5).\n  WAS: loop `listArtifacts(actor)`, read `sharedScope(art._id, art.userId)` per artifact.\n    BLOCKER A - a peer's ORG-VISIBLE artifact resolved to `usr.<peer>`: that peer's ENTIRE\n    owner-shared namespace, spanning apps the caller cannot see and collections the visible\n    artifact never names. Artifact visibility is not entitlement to its owner's rows. A read rung\n    that can enumerate a colleague's collections is an enumeration primitive, not a reuse ladder.\n    BLOCKER B - ambiguity decided per ARTIFACT over data scoped per OWNER: one namespace read N\n    times, N identical answers counted as N sources. Any owner of a SECOND app - holding anything\n    at all - was permanently refused `compose_ambiguous_collection`, with candidates naming apps\n    that did not hold the collection.\n  NOW: `ownerSharedScope(actor.userId)` - the acting user's own namespace, from the verified actor\n  and nothing else. Exactly the scope their own apps read and write through the served plane, so\n  the rung grants no reach they did not already have and none over anybody else's rows.\n  ONE SCOPE MEANS NO AMBIGUITY TO REPORT: `AppCollectionRead.ambiguous_collection` and the\n  `compose_ambiguous_collection` code are GONE (`code` is a free string on the wire - not a break).\n  ACCEPTED + REVIEW-DATED (Rule 10): a COLLEAGUE'S app data is now out of reach even when the app is\n  shared org-wide. The store has no per-app dimension to grant, so the narrow version is not\n  expressible today. Review by 2026-11-20.\n\n  integrations/action-parametrize.ts - D1 IS NOW AN ALLOWLIST, AND ONE PREDICATE.\n  WAS: a blocklist (\"fill anything that is not `targeting`\") reading slots off `httpConfig`. An\n  automation-backed action HAS no httpConfig, so `argSlotsOf` returned {} and EVERY argument read\n  `unused` - a model was free to choose which resource a WRITE acted on. That is the sentence the\n  module header says this product must never be able to say.\n  NOW: `mayBeModelFilled(action, name)` - fillable iff the action cannot write (a literal\n  `mutates: false`) or the argument lands in the BODY. Shared by the rung's pre-filter AND the\n  `targeting` check, so filter and suite cannot drift. `ArgSlot` gains `unknown` (no request to\n  read) as distinct from `unused` (a request that does not name it): \"cannot see where it goes\" and\n  \"goes nowhere\" are opposite conclusions, and collapsing them is what skipped a whole backing type.\n\n  integrations/integration-achieve.ts - THE COMPOSE RUNG IS NOT ENTERED FOR A WRITE.\n  WAS: entered whenever the goal had residue, write or not; a model's plan then refused the WHOLE\n  call. A `mutates` action executing under a standing human approval became model-dependent to run.\n  NOW: `mutates === false` is the FIRST line of `planComposition` - before the residue scan, the\n  collection listing, the allowance check and any model turn. A rung that can only ADD an answer\n  must never be able to SUBTRACT one. `composed_write_refused` no longer exists, and `read_only`\n  left `verifyComposePlan`: whether an ACTION may be composed over is a property of the CALL SITE,\n  not of the plan, and two statements of one rule where only one can fire is what caused this.\n\nPROOF (mutation, not inspection). Deleting the tenancy filter at the single query-binding point\n(`appId: scope.scopeKey`) reds the isolation suite in BOTH directions - rows via `list`, names via\n`listCollections`. Isolation tests 5 (peer's org-visible artifact) and 6 (owner with two apps) were\nverified RED against the unfixed binding before the fix existed. Suite:\napi/tests/security/achieve-compose-isolation.test.ts.",
      "rawText": "CORRECTION 2026-08-20b (S5 verification round two) - THE COMPOSE RUNG'S UNIT IS THE OWNER.\nSupersedes four claims in the AS-BUILT note above. Decision journal: D-S5-1. No new module, no new\ncollection, no new scope key: the same `usr.<owner>` key that already existed, bound correctly.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG, AND IT WAS ONE MISTAKE FOUR TIMES: a decision scoped to a unit the thing decided\nabout does not have.\n\n  data/collections-engine.ts - THE FACT THAT SETTLES IT. `sharedScope(appId, ownerUserId)` returns\n  `{ scopeKey: 'usr.<ownerUserId>', appId }`, and `Scope.appId` IS NEVER PART OF ANY QUERY: `docId`\n  and all six reads bind on `scope.scopeKey` alone. Shared `app_data` therefore has NO artifact\n  dimension, and \"the collections of app X\" is not a question this store can answer.\n  NEW: `ownerSharedScope(ownerUserId)` states the per-owner unit in the type, so a caller stops\n  reconstructing it from an `appId` the store ignores. `sharedScope` stays for the serving planes,\n  which genuinely hold an app and pass it through.\n\n  server.ts, the `appCollections` binding - REPLACED, and this is the tenancy decision (Rule 5).\n  WAS: loop `listArtifacts(actor)`, read `sharedScope(art._id, art.userId)` per artifact.\n    BLOCKER A - a peer's ORG-VISIBLE artifact resolved to `usr.<peer>`: that peer's ENTIRE\n    owner-shared namespace, spanning apps the caller cannot see and collections the visible\n    artifact never names. Artifact visibility is not entitlement to its owner's rows. A read rung\n    that can enumerate a colleague's collections is an enumeration primitive, not a reuse ladder.\n    BLOCKER B - ambiguity decided per ARTIFACT over data scoped per OWNER: one namespace read N\n    times, N identical answers counted as N sources. Any owner of a SECOND app - holding anything\n    at all - was permanently refused `compose_ambiguous_collection`, with candidates naming apps\n    that did not hold the collection.\n  NOW: `ownerSharedScope(actor.userId)` - the acting user's own namespace, from the verified actor\n  and nothing else. Exactly the scope their own apps read and write through the served plane, so\n  the rung grants no reach they did not already have and none over anybody else's rows.\n  ONE SCOPE MEANS NO AMBIGUITY TO REPORT: `AppCollectionRead.ambiguous_collection` and the\n  `compose_ambiguous_collection` code are GONE (`code` is a free string on the wire - not a break).\n  ACCEPTED + REVIEW-DATED (Rule 10): a COLLEAGUE'S app data is now out of reach even when the app is\n  shared org-wide. The store has no per-app dimension to grant, so the narrow version is not\n  expressible today. Review by 2026-11-20.\n\n  integrations/action-parametrize.ts - D1 IS NOW AN ALLOWLIST, AND ONE PREDICATE.\n  WAS: a blocklist (\"fill anything that is not `targeting`\") reading slots off `httpConfig`. An\n  automation-backed action HAS no httpConfig, so `argSlotsOf` returned {} and EVERY argument read\n  `unused` - a model was free to choose which resource a WRITE acted on. That is the sentence the\n  module header says this product must never be able to say.\n  NOW: `mayBeModelFilled(action, name)` - fillable iff the action cannot write (a literal\n  `mutates: false`) or the argument lands in the BODY. Shared by the rung's pre-filter AND the\n  `targeting` check, so filter and suite cannot drift. `ArgSlot` gains `unknown` (no request to\n  read) as distinct from `unused` (a request that does not name it): \"cannot see where it goes\" and\n  \"goes nowhere\" are opposite conclusions, and collapsing them is what skipped a whole backing type.\n\n  integrations/integration-achieve.ts - THE COMPOSE RUNG IS NOT ENTERED FOR A WRITE.\n  WAS: entered whenever the goal had residue, write or not; a model's plan then refused the WHOLE\n  call. A `mutates` action executing under a standing human approval became model-dependent to run.\n  NOW: `mutates === false` is the FIRST line of `planComposition` - before the residue scan, the\n  collection listing, the allowance check and any model turn. A rung that can only ADD an answer\n  must never be able to SUBTRACT one. `composed_write_refused` no longer exists, and `read_only`\n  left `verifyComposePlan`: whether an ACTION may be composed over is a property of the CALL SITE,\n  not of the plan, and two statements of one rule where only one can fire is what caused this.\n\nPROOF (mutation, not inspection). Deleting the tenancy filter at the single query-binding point\n(`appId: scope.scopeKey`) reds the isolation suite in BOTH directions - rows via `list`, names via\n`listCollections`. Isolation tests 5 (peer's org-visible artifact) and 6 (owner with two apps) were\nverified RED against the unfixed binding before the fix existed. Suite:\napi/tests/security/achieve-compose-isolation.test.ts.",
      "originalText": "CORRECTION 2026-08-20b (S5 verification round two) - THE COMPOSE RUNG'S UNIT IS THE OWNER.\nSupersedes four claims in the AS-BUILT note above. Decision journal: D-S5-1. No new module, no new\ncollection, no new scope key: the same `usr.<owner>` key that already existed, bound correctly.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG, AND IT WAS ONE MISTAKE FOUR TIMES: a decision scoped to a unit the thing decided\nabout does not have.\n\n  data/collections-engine.ts - THE FACT THAT SETTLES IT. `sharedScope(appId, ownerUserId)` returns\n  `{ scopeKey: 'usr.<ownerUserId>', appId }`, and `Scope.appId` IS NEVER PART OF ANY QUERY: `docId`\n  and all six reads bind on `scope.scopeKey` alone. Shared `app_data` therefore has NO artifact\n  dimension, and \"the collections of app X\" is not a question this store can answer.\n  NEW: `ownerSharedScope(ownerUserId)` states the per-owner unit in the type, so a caller stops\n  reconstructing it from an `appId` the store ignores. `sharedScope` stays for the serving planes,\n  which genuinely hold an app and pass it through.\n\n  server.ts, the `appCollections` binding - REPLACED, and this is the tenancy decision (Rule 5).\n  WAS: loop `listArtifacts(actor)`, read `sharedScope(art._id, art.userId)` per artifact.\n    BLOCKER A - a peer's ORG-VISIBLE artifact resolved to `usr.<peer>`: that peer's ENTIRE\n    owner-shared namespace, spanning apps the caller cannot see and collections the visible\n    artifact never names. Artifact visibility is not entitlement to its owner's rows. A read rung\n    that can enumerate a colleague's collections is an enumeration primitive, not a reuse ladder.\n    BLOCKER B - ambiguity decided per ARTIFACT over data scoped per OWNER: one namespace read N\n    times, N identical answers counted as N sources. Any owner of a SECOND app - holding anything\n    at all - was permanently refused `compose_ambiguous_collection`, with candidates naming apps\n    that did not hold the collection.\n  NOW: `ownerSharedScope(actor.userId)` - the acting user's own namespace, from the verified actor\n  and nothing else. Exactly the scope their own apps read and write through the served plane, so\n  the rung grants no reach they did not already have and none over anybody else's rows.\n  ONE SCOPE MEANS NO AMBIGUITY TO REPORT: `AppCollectionRead.ambiguous_collection` and the\n  `compose_ambiguous_collection` code are GONE (`code` is a free string on the wire - not a break).\n  ACCEPTED + REVIEW-DATED (Rule 10): a COLLEAGUE'S app data is now out of reach even when the app is\n  shared org-wide. The store has no per-app dimension to grant, so the narrow version is not\n  expressible today. Review by 2026-11-20.\n\n  integrations/action-parametrize.ts - D1 IS NOW AN ALLOWLIST, AND ONE PREDICATE.\n  WAS: a blocklist (\"fill anything that is not `targeting`\") reading slots off `httpConfig`. An\n  automation-backed action HAS no httpConfig, so `argSlotsOf` returned {} and EVERY argument read\n  `unused` - a model was free to choose which resource a WRITE acted on. That is the sentence the\n  module header says this product must never be able to say.\n  NOW: `mayBeModelFilled(action, name)` - fillable iff the action cannot write (a literal\n  `mutates: false`) or the argument lands in the BODY. Shared by the rung's pre-filter AND the\n  `targeting` check, so filter and suite cannot drift. `ArgSlot` gains `unknown` (no request to\n  read) as distinct from `unused` (a request that does not name it): \"cannot see where it goes\" and\n  \"goes nowhere\" are opposite conclusions, and collapsing them is what skipped a whole backing type.\n\n  integrations/integration-achieve.ts - THE COMPOSE RUNG IS NOT ENTERED FOR A WRITE.\n  WAS: entered whenever the goal had residue, write or not; a model's plan then refused the WHOLE\n  call. A `mutates` action executing under a standing human approval became model-dependent to run.\n  NOW: `mutates === false` is the FIRST line of `planComposition` - before the residue scan, the\n  collection listing, the allowance check and any model turn. A rung that can only ADD an answer\n  must never be able to SUBTRACT one. `composed_write_refused` no longer exists, and `read_only`\n  left `verifyComposePlan`: whether an ACTION may be composed over is a property of the CALL SITE,\n  not of the plan, and two statements of one rule where only one can fire is what caused this.\n\nPROOF (mutation, not inspection). Deleting the tenancy filter at the single query-binding point\n(`appId: scope.scopeKey`) reds the isolation suite in BOTH directions - rows via `list`, names via\n`listCollections`. Isolation tests 5 (peer's org-visible artifact) and 6 (owner with two apps) were\nverified RED against the unfixed binding before the fix existed. Suite:\napi/tests/security/achieve-compose-isolation.test.ts."
