      "text": "CORRECTION 2026-08-20c (S4+S5 verification round three) - A RUNG MAY ONLY ADD AN ANSWER.\nSupersedes the parametrize-refusal edge in the AS-BUILT note above. Journal: D-S4-2 / D-S5-2.\n\nTHE ONE RULE THIS ROUND ENFORCES, on every rung, for good:\n    a rung may ADD a cheaper way to answer; it may NEVER take away an answer the product\n    already gave under a standing human approval.\nRound two applied it to COMPOSE (a write never enters the rung). It was still broken on the\ntwo rungs below, in two different ways, and both are fixed here.\n\n1. PARAMETRIZE NO LONGER REFUSES THE CALL.\n   WAS:  verifyPlannedArgs fails -> return refused('parametrize_refused') -> NOTHING IS SENT.\n         An achieve that executed before this slice - caller's own args, human-trusted action,\n         standing approval behind any write - stopped executing because a model wrote a bad\n         argument.\n   NOW:  verifyPlannedArgs fails -> ladder.push({rung:'parametrize', verdict:'refused',\n         violations}) -> FALL THROUGH to the rung below, with `args` still exactly callerArgs.\n         verdict.args is null on a failed verdict, so not one model-supplied value survives -\n         including one that would have passed on its own.\n   EDGE REMOVED from the map: parametrize --refused--> (wire refusal).\n   EDGE ADDED:                parametrize --discarded--> reuse (the execute that always worked).\n   REFUSAL CODE REMOVED:      `parametrize_refused` is gone from AchieveRefusalCode. Nothing can\n                              emit it. A code that cannot occur is documentation that lies.\n\n2. COMPOSE IS A POST-STAGE, NOT AN ERROR BOUNDARY.\n   WAS:  execute -> (compose plan in hand) -> read the caller's collection -> rowsOf(data).\n         A failed execute carries NO data (action-executor.ts returns {success:false, status,\n         code, error} for a non-2xx), so rowsOf answered `unshaped` and the caller was told\n         \"the action returned no list to compose over\" - or compose_unknown_collection, since\n         the collection was read FIRST. A remote 500 is an answer ABOUT THE REMOTE SYSTEM and\n         POST /execute has always returned it whole; the wrapper named the wrong system.\n   NOW:  execute -> if (!out.value.success) return the `executed` arm VERBATIM, before the\n         collection is read, with compose recorded `skipped`. The route maps it exactly as it\n         always did: unknown_action -> 404, awaiting_consent -> the 403 envelope, else result.\n\n3. THE COLLECTION CAP IS SIGNALLED, AND PINNED (see 05-data-model for the wire shape).\n   composeRows still builds its key set from collectionRows.slice(0, COMPOSE_MAX_COLLECTION_ROWS\n   = 5000), but the summary now says so: collectionScanned + collectionTruncated, derived from\n   the WORK DONE (collectionRows.length > considered.length) rather than from the constant - so\n   deleting the cap makes the flag read `false` and reds the suite, which it did not before.\n\nUNCHANGED, AND RE-CHECKED THIS ROUND: the PICK is still lexical (matchActionForGoal, one call\nsite, neither rung handed definition.actions); there is still exactly ONE call to the gated\nexecutor from integration-achieve.ts; neither rung module can execute or approve anything; the\nappCollections binding is still ownerSharedScope(actor.userId) and nothing else.\n\nMUTATION SWEEP (39 mutants, source-side, each restored byte-identical): all red. Two SURVIVORS\nfound and closed with tests - argSlotsOf's \"targeting wins\" rule (one name in BOTH path and\nbody: on a write, a body template echoing {{numero}} would have laundered the path occurrence\nand made the resource selector model-fillable), and composeRows' COLLECTION-side String(k)\ncoercion. One further survivor recorded as a dismissal: deleting the parametrize rung's\nbinding-not-granted skip is safe (an empty allow-list fails closed in assertOriginAllowed), and\nwhat it really costs is a model call paid for to learn nothing - which is what its test asserts.",
      "rawText": "CORRECTION 2026-08-20c (S4+S5 verification round three) - A RUNG MAY ONLY ADD AN ANSWER.\nSupersedes the parametrize-refusal edge in the AS-BUILT note above. Journal: D-S4-2 / D-S5-2.\n\nTHE ONE RULE THIS ROUND ENFORCES, on every rung, for good:\n    a rung may ADD a cheaper way to answer; it may NEVER take away an answer the product\n    already gave under a standing human approval.\nRound two applied it to COMPOSE (a write never enters the rung). It was still broken on the\ntwo rungs below, in two different ways, and both are fixed here.\n\n1. PARAMETRIZE NO LONGER REFUSES THE CALL.\n   WAS:  verifyPlannedArgs fails -> return refused('parametrize_refused') -> NOTHING IS SENT.\n         An achieve that executed before this slice - caller's own args, human-trusted action,\n         standing approval behind any write - stopped executing because a model wrote a bad\n         argument.\n   NOW:  verifyPlannedArgs fails -> ladder.push({rung:'parametrize', verdict:'refused',\n         violations}) -> FALL THROUGH to the rung below, with `args` still exactly callerArgs.\n         verdict.args is null on a failed verdict, so not one model-supplied value survives -\n         including one that would have passed on its own.\n   EDGE REMOVED from the map: parametrize --refused--> (wire refusal).\n   EDGE ADDED:                parametrize --discarded--> reuse (the execute that always worked).\n   REFUSAL CODE REMOVED:      `parametrize_refused` is gone from AchieveRefusalCode. Nothing can\n                              emit it. A code that cannot occur is documentation that lies.\n\n2. COMPOSE IS A POST-STAGE, NOT AN ERROR BOUNDARY.\n   WAS:  execute -> (compose plan in hand) -> read the caller's collection -> rowsOf(data).\n         A failed execute carries NO data (action-executor.ts returns {success:false, status,\n         code, error} for a non-2xx), so rowsOf answered `unshaped` and the caller was told\n         \"the action returned no list to compose over\" - or compose_unknown_collection, since\n         the collection was read FIRST. A remote 500 is an answer ABOUT THE REMOTE SYSTEM and\n         POST /execute has always returned it whole; the wrapper named the wrong system.\n   NOW:  execute -> if (!out.value.success) return the `executed` arm VERBATIM, before the\n         collection is read, with compose recorded `skipped`. The route maps it exactly as it\n         always did: unknown_action -> 404, awaiting_consent -> the 403 envelope, else result.\n\n3. THE COLLECTION CAP IS SIGNALLED, AND PINNED (see 05-data-model for the wire shape).\n   composeRows still builds its key set from collectionRows.slice(0, COMPOSE_MAX_COLLECTION_ROWS\n   = 5000), but the summary now says so: collectionScanned + collectionTruncated, derived from\n   the WORK DONE (collectionRows.length > considered.length) rather than from the constant - so\n   deleting the cap makes the flag read `false` and reds the suite, which it did not before.\n\nUNCHANGED, AND RE-CHECKED THIS ROUND: the PICK is still lexical (matchActionForGoal, one call\nsite, neither rung handed definition.actions); there is still exactly ONE call to the gated\nexecutor from integration-achieve.ts; neither rung module can execute or approve anything; the\nappCollections binding is still ownerSharedScope(actor.userId) and nothing else.\n\nMUTATION SWEEP (39 mutants, source-side, each restored byte-identical): all red. Two SURVIVORS\nfound and closed with tests - argSlotsOf's \"targeting wins\" rule (one name in BOTH path and\nbody: on a write, a body template echoing {{numero}} would have laundered the path occurrence\nand made the resource selector model-fillable), and composeRows' COLLECTION-side String(k)\ncoercion. One further survivor recorded as a dismissal: deleting the parametrize rung's\nbinding-not-granted skip is safe (an empty allow-list fails closed in assertOriginAllowed), and\nwhat it really costs is a model call paid for to learn nothing - which is what its test asserts.",
      "originalText": "CORRECTION 2026-08-20c (S4+S5 verification round three) - A RUNG MAY ONLY ADD AN ANSWER.\nSupersedes the parametrize-refusal edge in the AS-BUILT note above. Journal: D-S4-2 / D-S5-2.\n\nTHE ONE RULE THIS ROUND ENFORCES, on every rung, for good:\n    a rung may ADD a cheaper way to answer; it may NEVER take away an answer the product\n    already gave under a standing human approval.\nRound two applied it to COMPOSE (a write never enters the rung). It was still broken on the\ntwo rungs below, in two different ways, and both are fixed here.\n\n1. PARAMETRIZE NO LONGER REFUSES THE CALL.\n   WAS:  verifyPlannedArgs fails -> return refused('parametrize_refused') -> NOTHING IS SENT.\n         An achieve that executed before this slice - caller's own args, human-trusted action,\n         standing approval behind any write - stopped executing because a model wrote a bad\n         argument.\n   NOW:  verifyPlannedArgs fails -> ladder.push({rung:'parametrize', verdict:'refused',\n         violations}) -> FALL THROUGH to the rung below, with `args` still exactly callerArgs.\n         verdict.args is null on a failed verdict, so not one model-supplied value survives -\n         including one that would have passed on its own.\n   EDGE REMOVED from the map: parametrize --refused--> (wire refusal).\n   EDGE ADDED:                parametrize --discarded--> reuse (the execute that always worked).\n   REFUSAL CODE REMOVED:      `parametrize_refused` is gone from AchieveRefusalCode. Nothing can\n                              emit it. A code that cannot occur is documentation that lies.\n\n2. COMPOSE IS A POST-STAGE, NOT AN ERROR BOUNDARY.\n   WAS:  execute -> (compose plan in hand) -> read the caller's collection -> rowsOf(data).\n         A failed execute carries NO data (action-executor.ts returns {success:false, status,\n         code, error} for a non-2xx), so rowsOf answered `unshaped` and the caller was told\n         \"the action returned no list to compose over\" - or compose_unknown_collection, since\n         the collection was read FIRST. A remote 500 is an answer ABOUT THE REMOTE SYSTEM and\n         POST /execute has always returned it whole; the wrapper named the wrong system.\n   NOW:  execute -> if (!out.value.success) return the `executed` arm VERBATIM, before the\n         collection is read, with compose recorded `skipped`. The route maps it exactly as it\n         always did: unknown_action -> 404, awaiting_consent -> the 403 envelope, else result.\n\n3. THE COLLECTION CAP IS SIGNALLED, AND PINNED (see 05-data-model for the wire shape).\n   composeRows still builds its key set from collectionRows.slice(0, COMPOSE_MAX_COLLECTION_ROWS\n   = 5000), but the summary now says so: collectionScanned + collectionTruncated, derived from\n   the WORK DONE (collectionRows.length > considered.length) rather than from the constant - so\n   deleting the cap makes the flag read `false` and reds the suite, which it did not before.\n\nUNCHANGED, AND RE-CHECKED THIS ROUND: the PICK is still lexical (matchActionForGoal, one call\nsite, neither rung handed definition.actions); there is still exactly ONE call to the gated\nexecutor from integration-achieve.ts; neither rung module can execute or approve anything; the\nappCollections binding is still ownerSharedScope(actor.userId) and nothing else.\n\nMUTATION SWEEP (39 mutants, source-side, each restored byte-identical): all red. Two SURVIVORS\nfound and closed with tests - argSlotsOf's \"targeting wins\" rule (one name in BOTH path and\nbody: on a write, a body template echoing {{numero}} would have laundered the path occurrence\nand made the resource selector model-fillable), and composeRows' COLLECTION-side String(k)\ncoercion. One further survivor recorded as a dismissal: deleting the parametrize rung's\nbinding-not-granted skip is safe (an empty allow-list fails closed in assertOriginAllowed), and\nwhat it really costs is a model call paid for to learn nothing - which is what its test asserts."
