      "text": "CORRECTION 2026-08-20d (S4+S5 verification round FOUR) - THE COMPOSE RUNG STOPS SUBTRACTING, AND\nTHE MAP STOPS CLAIMING IT ALREADY DID. Supersedes note (c)'s closing claim and the compose-refusal\nedges in the AS-BUILT note. Decision journal: D-S5-3.\n\nWHAT NOTE (c) ASSERTED, AND WHAT THE CODE DID\n  (c) stated the rule \"a rung may ADD a cheaper way to answer; it may NEVER take away an answer the\n  product already gave\", and said it was enforced \"on every rung, for good\". The code disagreed on\n  THREE paths, and two of them were worse than the parametrize defect (c) had just fixed, because\n  they refused AFTER the remote call had already been made AND HAD SUCCEEDED:\n\n    execute (200, rows in hand) -> read the caller's collection -> unknown_collection\n        -> return refused('compose_unknown_collection')      <- the 200 is discarded\n    execute (200, rows in hand) -> rowsOf(data) is `unshaped`\n        -> return refused('compose_unshaped_result')         <- the 200 is discarded\n    the plan verdict fails, BEFORE the execute\n        -> return refused('compose_refused')                 <- the call never runs at all\n\n  The first two spend the side effect and then throw away the result: the product performs the\n  caller's work, gets a good answer back, and hands over a refusal. The third does not spend the\n  side effect, but it still ends a call that EXECUTED before this slice existed - so (c)'s defence\n  of it (\"decided BEFORE anything runs, about a call the caller has not yet made\") was not a defence\n  either. The caller HAS made the call; what has not happened yet is the request.\n\nTHE MAP AS IT NOW IS\n  EDGES REMOVED:  compose --unknown_collection--> (wire refusal)\n                  compose --unshaped_result----> (wire refusal)\n                  compose --plan refused-------> (wire refusal)\n  EDGE ADDED:     compose --did not apply------> reuse (the executed answer, unchanged)\n  There is now exactly ONE exit from `runMatchedAction` for an admitted call that was not composed,\n  and it always carries `out.value` - the executor's own result. \"The answer survives\" is therefore\n  a property of the SHAPE of the function rather than of four separate branches each remembering to\n  preserve it: `applyComposition` returns the composed rows or NULL, and null falls through.\n\nREFUSAL CODES: THE LADDER NOW INTRODUCES NONE AT ALL.\n  `compose_refused`, `compose_unknown_collection` and `compose_unshaped_result` are removed from\n  AchieveRefusalCode, joining `parametrize_refused` (note c) and `composed_write_refused` +\n  `compose_ambiguous_collection` (note b). The union is back to exactly the THIRTEEN author-arm\n  codes that pre-date the ladder, every one of which refuses a call that could not have run anyway.\n  The wire carries `code` as a free string and the enum was never published, so no schema breaks.\n\nWHAT A CALLER SEES INSTEAD: `outcome: 'executed'`, `result` = the action's own answer, and the step\n  { rung: 'compose', verdict: 'refused', detail, violations? } on the ladder beside it. The rung is\n  diagnosable from the answer it did NOT prevent - which is exactly the shape (c) built for the\n  parametrize case and did not carry down to this one.\n\nUNCHANGED, RE-CHECKED A FOURTH TIME: the PICK is still lexical (`matchActionForGoal`, one\ndeclaration + one call site, neither rung handed `definition.actions`); there is still exactly ONE\ncall to the gated executor from `integration-achieve.ts`; neither rung module can execute or approve\nanything; the `appCollections` binding is still `ownerSharedScope(actor.userId)` and nothing else.\n\nMUTATION SWEEP, ROUND FOUR. 54 mutants in the first pass over the six source files, 31 more in a\nconfirmation pass over the fixed code (one written for every fix this round makes); each applied to\nthe SOURCE, run against all four slice suites, restored and verified byte-identical. SIX real\nunfailable assertions were found and are now closed by tests - the count is six, not the four the\nreview round expected, and it is stated rather than rounded down:\n  1. COMPOSE_MAX_ITEMS was never pinned to a literal, so the cap could drift to any value and stay\n     green. Its SIBLING constant was pinned in round three; the same defect was left standing here.\n  2. The \"compose stage reaches no store\" static guard searched for a CommonJS `require(...)` shape\n     an ESM file cannot produce, while the real hazard - `import { CollectionsEngine }` - passed it.\n  3. argSlotsOf's QUERY slot had no case where the slot was observable (D1 names path AND query;\n     only the path and header slots had fixtures).\n  4. `contains` could become `startsWith` unnoticed - every probe compared a prefix.\n  5. `String(field ?? '')` could lose its coercion - the only field-absent assertion used value ''.\n  6. `ends_with` could become `includes`, and the FIRST PASS DID NOT ASK: no such mutant was written,\n     so its absence of a result read as coverage. The confirmation pass wrote one and it survived.\nFOUR DISMISSALS in writing (docs/findings.md): the caller-args spread order (a true equivalent\nmutant, unchanged from note b); renderTemplate's unsupplied-placeholder fallback (DEAD CODE at every\ncall site, so no test can reach it); verifyComposePlan's missing-`collection` shape check, which\nchanges no verdict but does change the CHECK LIST - closed by a test rather than dismissed; and the\nisolation suite's no-existence-oracle EQUALITY, which is a regression guard rather than a killable\nassertion (applyComposition cannot reach another tenant's scope, so it holds no fact to leak; a\nmutant fabricating a difference out of actor.orgId survived, correctly, because it did not vary with\nthe thing it claimed to disclose). What IS killable, and is now asserted, is the SHAPE that makes the\noracle impossible: AppCollectionRead's not-found answer is a bare tag, with no payload for such a\nfact to travel in.",
      "rawText": "CORRECTION 2026-08-20d (S4+S5 verification round FOUR) - THE COMPOSE RUNG STOPS SUBTRACTING, AND\nTHE MAP STOPS CLAIMING IT ALREADY DID. Supersedes note (c)'s closing claim and the compose-refusal\nedges in the AS-BUILT note. Decision journal: D-S5-3.\n\nWHAT NOTE (c) ASSERTED, AND WHAT THE CODE DID\n  (c) stated the rule \"a rung may ADD a cheaper way to answer; it may NEVER take away an answer the\n  product already gave\", and said it was enforced \"on every rung, for good\". The code disagreed on\n  THREE paths, and two of them were worse than the parametrize defect (c) had just fixed, because\n  they refused AFTER the remote call had already been made AND HAD SUCCEEDED:\n\n    execute (200, rows in hand) -> read the caller's collection -> unknown_collection\n        -> return refused('compose_unknown_collection')      <- the 200 is discarded\n    execute (200, rows in hand) -> rowsOf(data) is `unshaped`\n        -> return refused('compose_unshaped_result')         <- the 200 is discarded\n    the plan verdict fails, BEFORE the execute\n        -> return refused('compose_refused')                 <- the call never runs at all\n\n  The first two spend the side effect and then throw away the result: the product performs the\n  caller's work, gets a good answer back, and hands over a refusal. The third does not spend the\n  side effect, but it still ends a call that EXECUTED before this slice existed - so (c)'s defence\n  of it (\"decided BEFORE anything runs, about a call the caller has not yet made\") was not a defence\n  either. The caller HAS made the call; what has not happened yet is the request.\n\nTHE MAP AS IT NOW IS\n  EDGES REMOVED:  compose --unknown_collection--> (wire refusal)\n                  compose --unshaped_result----> (wire refusal)\n                  compose --plan refused-------> (wire refusal)\n  EDGE ADDED:     compose --did not apply------> reuse (the executed answer, unchanged)\n  There is now exactly ONE exit from `run