      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "text": "CORRECTION 2026-08-20e (S4+S5 verification round FIVE) - A RUNG CANNOT SUBTRACT BY THROWING EITHER.\nExtends notes (c) and (d) above; neither is wrong now, both were incomplete. Journal: D-S5-4.\n\nWHAT NOTES (c) AND (d) BOTH MISSED. They removed refusal EDGES from the map and then declared the\nrule enforced. An edge is a RETURN. An answer can also be taken away by an EXCEPTION, and an\nexception is not on this map at all - which is exactly how it survived two rounds of correcting it.\n\nTHE EDGE THAT WAS NOT DRAWN:\n\n    routes/integrations --achieve--> integrations/integration-achieve\n                                          |\n                                          |  execute (ONE call, C2's gate inside it)\n                                          v\n                                     the remote  ---- 200, rows in hand ---->\n                                          |\n                                          |  post-stage: appCollections.read(actor, name)\n                                          v\n                             server.ts binding --> data/collections-engine.list\n                                          |\n                                          |  MONGO QUERY. It can REJECT: dropped connection,\n                                          |  timeout, replica-set election.\n                                          X  <-- the rejection propagated straight back up\n                                                 through applyComposition, runMatchedAction and\n                                                 achieveIntegrationGoal into the route's error\n                                                 handler, and the caller got 500 + no processos.\n\nThe side effect was SPENT and the rows were IN HAND when that happened. A refusal, a swallowed\nanswer and an exception are three exits from one wrong idea: a post-stage does not get to decide the\nanswer no longer exists.\n\nTHE SHAPE THAT CLOSES IT - each stage splits in two, and the split is the fix:\n\n    WORKER (may throw, writes NOTHING)          RECORDER (writes the ONE ladder step)\n    ---------------------------------           -------------------------------------\n    attemptComposition(...)            ---->    applyComposition(...)\n      reads appCollections                        try { worker } catch -> fixed-text step\n      calls rowsOf / composeRows                  one push per outcome, then null-or-rows\n    draftCompositionPlan(...)          ---->    planComposition(...)\n      lists collections, asks the model           same, then none-or-plan\n\nBecause the worker writes nothing, a throw cannot leave a half-written ladder behind - that is true\nby CONSTRUCTION rather than by reading every branch and checking each push is followed by a return.\n\nBOTH STAGES, NOT JUST THE POST-STAGE. planComposition runs BEFORE the execute, so a rejection there\ndid not discard a spent 200 - it stopped the request going out at all, and an achieve that had been\nexecuting since long before this slice 500'd because a rung ABOVE the one that answers it could not\ndo its optional extra work.\n\nWHAT A THROW PUTS ON THE WIRE IS FIXED TEXT. The error's own message is logged for an operator and\nnever travels: a driver's message names a namespace, a replica-set host and a query shape, and\nladder[].detail is a caller-facing field on a public capability endpoint.\n\nALSO CORRECTED ON THIS MAP'S SEAM LABELS: the `mutates` gate on the compose entry is `!== false`\n(fail-closed) and that reading is now OBSERVABLE at the seam. definitions.ts builds a package\ndefinition's actions as `config.actions ?? []` off an unvalidated config.json, and\ndefinition-store.ts persists an agent-authored action verbatim, so an action with NO `mutates` key\nreaches this rung in production. Reading the gate as `=== true` would let it into the compose rung.",
      "rawText": "CORRECTION 2026-08-20e (S4+S5 verification round FIVE) - A RUNG CANNOT SUBTRACT BY THROWING EITHER.\nExtends notes (c) and (d) above; neither is wrong now, both were incomplete. Journal: D-S5-4.\n\nWHAT NOTES (c) AND (d) BOTH MISSED. They removed refusal EDGES from the map and then declared the\nrule enforced. An edge is a RETURN. An answer can also be taken away by an EXCEPTION, and an\nexception is not on this map at all - which is exactly how it survived two rounds of correcting it.\n\nTHE EDGE THAT WAS NOT DRAWN:\n\n    routes/integrations --achieve--> integrations/integration-achieve\n                                          |\n                                          |  execute (ONE call, C2's gate inside it)\n                                          v\n                                     the remote  ---- 200, rows in hand ---->\n                                          |\n                                          |  post-stage: appCollections.read(actor, name)\n                                          v\n                             server.ts binding --> data/collections-engine.list\n                                          |\n                                          |  MONGO QUERY. It can REJECT: dropped connection,\n                                          |  timeout, replica-set election.\n                                          X  <-- the rejection propagated straight back up\n                                                 through applyComposition, runMatchedAction and\n                                                 achieveIntegrationGoal into the route's error\n                                                 handler, and the caller got 500 + no processos.\n\nThe side effect was SPENT and the rows were IN HAND when that happened. A refusal, a swallowed\nanswer and an exception are three exits from one wrong idea: a post-stage does not get to decide the\nanswer no longer exists.\n\nTHE SHAPE THAT CLOSES IT - each stage splits in two, and the split is the fix:\n\n    WORKER (may throw, writes NOTHING)          RECORDER (writes the ONE ladder step)\n    ---------------------------------           -------------------------------------\n    attemptComposition(...)            ---->    applyComposition(...)\n      reads appCollections                        try { worker } catch -> fixed-text step\n      calls rowsOf / composeRows                  one push per outcome, then null-or-rows\n    draftCompositionPlan(...)          ---->    planComposition(...)\n      lists collections, asks the model           same, then none-or-plan\n\nBecause the worker writes nothing, a throw cannot leave a half-written ladder behind - that is true\nby CONSTRUCTION rather than by reading every branch and checking each push is followed by a return.\n\nBOTH STAGES, NOT JUST THE POST-STAGE. planComposition runs BEFORE the execute, so a rejection there\ndid not discard a spent 200 - it stopped the request going out at all, and an achieve that had been\nexecuting since long before this slice 500'd because a rung ABOVE the one that answers it could not\ndo its optional extra work.\n\nWHAT A THROW PUTS ON THE WIRE IS FIXED TEXT. The error's own message is logged for an operator and\nnever travels: a driver's message names a namespace, a replica-set host and a query shape, and\nladder[].detail is a caller-facing field on a public capability endpoint.\n\nALSO CORRECTED ON THIS MAP'S SEAM LABELS: the `mutates` gate on the compose entry is `!== false`\n(fail-closed) and that reading is now OBSERVABLE at the seam. definitions.ts builds a package\ndefinition's actions as `config.actions ?? []` off an unvalidated config.json, and\ndefinition-store.ts persists an agent-authored action verbatim, so an action with NO `mutates` key\nreaches this rung in production. Reading the gate as `=== true` would let it into the compose rung.",
      "originalText": "CORRECTION 2026-08-20e (S4+S5 verification round FIVE) - A RUNG CANNOT SUBTRACT BY THROWING EITHER.\nExtends notes (c) and (d) above; neither is wrong now, both were incomplete. Journal: D-S5-4.\n\nWHAT NOTES (c) AND (d) BOTH MISSED. They removed refusal EDGES from the map and then declared the\nrule enforced. An edge is a RETURN. An answer can also be taken away by an EXCEPTION, and an\nexception is not on this map at all - which is exactly how it survived two rounds of correcting it.\n\nTHE EDGE THAT WAS NOT DRAWN:\n\n    routes/integrations --achieve--> integrations/integration-achieve\n                                          |\n                                          |  execute (ONE call, C2's gate inside it)\n                                          v\n                                     the remote  ---- 200, rows in hand ---->\n                                          |\n                                          |  post-stage: appCollections.read(actor, name)\n                                          v\n                             server.ts binding --> data/collections-engine.list\n                                          |\n                                          |  MONGO QUERY. It can REJECT: dropped connection,\n                                          |  timeout, replica-set election.\n                                          X  <-- the rejection propagated straight back up\n                                                 through applyComposition, runMatchedAction and\n                                                 achieveIntegrationGoal into the route's error\n                                                 handler, and the caller got 500 + no processos.\n\nThe side effect was SPENT and the rows were IN HAND when that happened. A refusal, a swallowed\nanswer and an exception are three exits from one wrong idea: a post-stage does not get to decide the\nanswer no longer exists.\n\nTHE SHAPE THAT CLOSES IT - each stage splits in two, and the split is the fix:\n\n    WORKER (may throw, writes NOTHING)          RECORDER (writes the ONE ladder step)\n    ---------------------------------           -------------------------------------\n    attemptComposition(...)            ---->    applyComposition(...)\n      reads appCollections                        try { worker } catch -> fixed-text step\n      calls rowsOf / composeRows                  one push per outcome, then null-or-rows\n    draftCompositionPlan(...)          ---->    planComposition(...)\n      lists collections, asks the model           same, then none-or-plan\n\nBecause the worker writes nothing, a throw cannot leave a half-written ladder behind - that is true\nby CONSTRUCTION rather than by reading every branch and checking each push is followed by a return.\n\nBOTH STAGES, NOT JUST THE POST-STAGE. planComposition runs BEFORE the execute, so a rejection there\ndid not discard a spent 200 - it stopped the request going out at all, and an achieve that had been\nexecuting since long before this slice 500'd because a rung ABOVE the one that answers it could not\ndo its optional extra work.\n\nWHAT A THROW PUTS ON THE WIRE IS FIXED TEXT. The error's own message is logged for an operator and\nnever travels: a driver's message names a namespace, a replica-set host and a query shape, and\nladder[].detail is a caller-facing field on a public capability endpoint.\n\nALSO CORRECTED ON THIS MAP'S SEAM LABELS: the `mutates` gate on the compose entry is `!== false`\n(fail-closed) and that reading is now OBSERVABLE at the seam. definitions.ts builds a package\ndefinition's actions as `config.actions ?? []` off an unvalidated config.json, and\ndefinition-store.ts persists an agent-authored action verbatim, so an action with NO `mutates` key\nreaches this rung in production. Reading the gate as `=== true` would let it into the compose rung."
