      "updated": 1756000000001,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "CORRECTION 2026-08-21g (S4+S5 verification round SEVEN) - THE PARAMETRIZE RUNG GETS THE\nWORKER/RECORDER SPLIT THE COMPOSE RUNG ALREADY HAD. Extends notes (c)-(f). Journal: D-S4-3/D-S5-6.\n\nWHAT THE MAP SHOWED, AND WHAT WAS WRONG WITH IT\n  Notes (e)-(f) drew the compose rung as a PAIR at each stage - a worker that touches a seam and may\n  throw, and a recorder that converts every outcome (a rejection included) into one ladder step -\n  and said that is what makes \"a rung cannot take an answer away\" true by construction.\n  The PARAMETRIZE rung was drawn as a single inline block, and that was accurate: it had no split.\n  Its seam calls sat in `runMatchedAction` inside no `try` at all.\n\n  integration-achieve.ts\n    runMatchedAction\n      RUNG 2  parametrize  --[ checkAllowance ]------> billing/ ......... MAY REJECT, UNCAUGHT\n                           --[ ctx.planStep ]--------> agents/ .......... MAY REJECT, UNCAUGHT\n      RUNG 1  execute -----> integration-capability -> action-executor\n      RUNG 3  compose  ----> planComposition/applyComposition (both split, both catch)\n\n  A rejection from either seam left runMatchedAction BEFORE the one gated execute, so the action\n  was never called: the caller's goal came back a 500 because a billing read or a socket blipped.\n  The compose rung throws AFTER the answer exists (round four's defect, fixed); this one threw\n  BEFORE it exists, which prevents the answer instead of destroying it.\n\nWHAT THE MAP SHOWS NOW\n  integration-achieve.ts\n    runMatchedAction\n      RUNG 2  parametrizeArgs         RECORDER  one try/catch, writes the ONE ladder step,\n                                                returns args - never a refusal, never a throw\n                `- draftParametrizedArgs  WORKER  touches checkAllowance + ctx.planStep +\n                                                  resolveCredentialEgressBinding, MAY throw,\n                                                  writes nothing\n      RUNG 1  execute (unchanged - still the ONE call to the gated executor)\n      RUNG 3  composeOver -> planComposition/draftCompositionPlan\n                          -> applyComposition/attemptComposition   (unchanged)\n\n  All three stages now have the same shape, and the module has exactly three places where a seam\n  rejection is converted into a ladder step.\n\nWHAT DID NOT MOVE\n  - the PICK is still `matchActionForGoal`, lexical and deterministic. No model reaches it.\n  - the ONE call to the gated executor is still one call, in the same place.\n  - `resolveCredentialEgressBinding` is NOT in the \"may throw\" set and the map should not draw it\n    there: it wraps its own body in a try and answers `refused` on any failure, deliberately, so a\n    resolver error can never WIDEN a binding. The cost is that a locked credential and a fallen-over\n    resolver arrive as one value, and the rung says one sentence for both rather than inventing a\n    distinction it does not have.\n\nWHAT ELSE ENTERS THE PROMPT, AND THROUGH WHAT\n  action-compose.ts gains `promptSafeFields`, applied in `draftCompositionPlan` to BOTH field sets\n  at the one place they are produced - so the array the prompt renders IS the array\n  verifyComposePlan enforces. Third-party-writable names (a remote API's JSON keys; app_data field\n  names, which pass no guard on any write path) are bounded to 100 names of at most 64 characters\n  and stripped of control characters and backticks before they reach a system prompt.",
      "rawText": "CORRECTION 2026-08-21g (S4+S5 verification round SEVEN) - THE PARAMETRIZE RUNG GETS THE\nWORKER/RECORDER SPLIT THE COMPOSE RUNG ALREADY HAD. Extends notes (c)-(f). Journal: D-S4-3/D-S5-6.\n\nWHAT THE MAP SHOWED, AND WHAT WAS WRONG WITH IT\n  Notes (e)-(f) drew the compose rung as a PAIR at each stage - a worker that touches a seam and may\n  throw, and a recorder that converts every outcome (a rejection included) into one ladder step -\n  and said that is what makes \"a rung cannot take an answer away\" true by construction.\n  The PARAMETRIZE rung was drawn as a single inline block, and that was accurate: it had no split.\n  Its seam calls sat in `runMatchedAction` inside no `try` at all.\n\n  integration-achieve.ts\n    runMatchedAction\n      RUNG 2  parametrize  --[ checkAllowance ]------> billing/ ......... MAY REJECT, UNCAUGHT\n                           --[ ctx.planStep ]--------> agents/ .......... MAY REJECT, UNCAUGHT\n      RUNG 1  execute -----> integration-capability -> action-executor\n      RUNG 3  compose  ----> planComposition/applyComposition (both split, both catch)\n\n  A rejection from either seam left runMatchedAction BEFORE the one gated execute, so the action\n  was never called: the caller's goal came back a 500 because a billing read or a socket blipped.\n  The compose rung throws AFTER the answer exists (round four's defect, fixed); this one threw\n  BEFORE it exists, which prevents the answer instead of destroying it.\n\nWHAT THE MAP SHOWS NOW\n  integration-achieve.ts\n    runMatchedAction\n      RUNG 2  parametrizeArgs         RECORDER  one try/catch, writes the ONE ladder step,\n                                                returns args - never a refusal, never a throw\n                `- draftParametrizedArgs  WORKER  touches checkAllowance + ctx.planStep +\n                                                  resolveCredentialEgressBinding, MAY throw,\n                                                  writes nothing\n      RUNG 1  execute (unchanged - still the ONE call to the gated executor)\n      RUNG 3  composeOver -> planComposition/draftCompositionPlan\n                          -> applyComposition/attemptComposition   (unchanged)\n\n  All three stages now have the same shape, and the module has exactly three places where a seam\n  rejection is converted into a ladder step.\n\nWHAT DID NOT MOVE\n  - the PICK is still `matchActionForGoal`, lexical and deterministic. No model reaches it.\n  - the ONE call to the gated executor is still one call, in the same place.\n  - `resolveCredentialEgressBinding` is NOT in the \"may throw\" set and the map should not draw it\n    there: it wraps its own body in a try and answers `refused` on any failure, deliberately, so a\n    resolver error can never WIDEN a binding. The cost is that a locked credential and a fallen-over\n    resolver arrive as one value, and the rung says one sentence for both rather than inventing a\n    distinction it does not have.\n\nWHAT ELSE ENTERS THE PROMPT, AND THROUGH WHAT\n  action-compose.ts gains `promptSafeFields`, applied in `draftCompositionPlan` to BOTH field sets\n  at the one place they are produced - so the array the prompt renders IS the array\n  verifyComposePlan enforces. Third-party-writable names (a remote API's JSON keys; app_data field\n  names, which pass no guard on any write path) are bounded to 100 names of at most 64 characters\n  and stripped of control characters and backticks before they reach a system prompt.",
      "originalText": "CORRECTION 2026-08-21g (S4+S5 verification round SEVEN) - THE PARAMETRIZE RUNG GETS THE\nWORKER/RECORDER SPLIT THE COMPOSE RUNG ALREADY HAD. Extends notes (c)-(f). Journal: D-S4-3/D-S5-6.\n\nWHAT THE MAP SHOWED, AND WHAT WAS WRONG WITH IT\n  Notes (e)-(f) drew the compose rung as a PAIR at each stage - a worker that touches a seam and may\n  throw, and a recorder that converts every outcome (a rejection included) into one ladder step -\n  and said that is what makes \"a rung cannot take an answer away\" true by construction.\n  The PARAMETRIZE rung was drawn as a single inline block, and that was accurate: it had no split.\n  Its seam calls sat in `runMatchedAction` inside no `try` at all.\n\n  integration-achieve.ts\n    runMatchedAction\n      RUNG 2  parametrize  --[ checkAllowance ]------> billing/ ......... MAY REJECT, UNCAUGHT\n                           --[ ctx.planStep ]--------> agents/ .......... MAY REJECT, UNCAUGHT\n      RUNG 1  execute -----> integration-capability -> action-executor\n      RUNG 3  compose  ----> planComposition/applyComposition (both split, both catch)\n\n  A rejection from either seam left runMatchedAction BEFORE the one gated execute, so the action\n  was never called: the caller's goal came back a 500 because a billing read or a socket blipped.\n  The compose rung throws AFTER the answer exists (round four's defect, fixed); this one threw\n  BEFORE it exists, which prevents the answer instead of destroying it.\n\nWHAT THE MAP SHOWS NOW\n  integration-achieve.ts\n    runMatchedAction\n      RUNG 2  parametrizeArgs         RECORDER  one try/catch, writes the ONE ladder step,\n                                                returns args - never a refusal, never a throw\n                `- draftParametrizedArgs  WORKER  touches checkAllowance + ctx.planStep +\n                                                  resolveCredentialEgressBinding, MAY throw,\n                                                  writes nothing\n      RUNG 1  execute (unchanged - still the ONE call to the gated executor)\n      RUNG 3  composeOver -> planComposition/draftCompositionPlan\n                          -> applyComposition/attemptComposition   (unchanged)\n\n  All three stages now have the same shape, and the module has exactly three places where a seam\n  rejection is converted into a ladder step.\n\nWHAT DID NOT MOVE\n  - the PICK is still `matchActionForGoal`, lexical and deterministic. No model reaches it.\n  - the ONE call to the gated executor is still one call, in the same place.\n  - `resolveCredentialEgressBinding` is NOT in the \"may throw\" set and the map should not draw it\n    there: it wraps its own body in a try and answers `refused` on any failure, deliberately, so a\n    resolver error can never WIDEN a binding. The cost is that a locked credential and a fallen-over\n    resolver arrive as one value, and the rung says one sentence for both rather than inventing a\n    distinction it does not have.\n\nWHAT ELSE ENTERS THE PROMPT, AND THROUGH WHAT\n  action-compose.ts gains `promptSafeFields`, applied in `draftCompositionPlan` to BOTH field sets\n  at the one place they are produced - so the array the prompt renders IS the array\n  verifyComposePlan enforces. Third-party-writable names (a remote API's JSON keys; app_data field\n  names, which pass no guard on any write path) are bounded to 100 names of at most 64 characters\n  and stripped of control characters and backticks before they reach a system prompt.",
