    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "asbuilt-20260820-s2-detail-surface",
      "x": 60,
      "y": 27100,
      "width": 1120,
      "height": 770.0,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-20 (slice S2, the integration DETAIL surface): one new read module on the api side,\nfour new modules on the web side, and no new state anywhere - S2 reads what S1 already records.\n\nNEW api/src/integrations/action-evidence-view.ts (tier 3) - the READ side of S1's\nintegration_action_evidence. A SEPARATE FILE from integration-capability.ts on purpose: that module is\nthe user-or-key capability core, and keeping them apart means nobody can make this collection\nkey-reachable by adding a field to a view the core already serves. It enforces TWO scopes:\n\n  1. THE ORG AND THE OWNER. S1's row is keyed by (orgId, ownerUserId, integrationKey, actionName) and\n     listForIntegration takes both identity terms; this module passes the CALLER'S OWN actor.orgId and\n     actor.userId, never an id read off a request. So neither another tenant's row nor a colleague's\n     is addressable at all. The consequence is deliberate: two members of one org see DIFFERENT\n     evidence for the same action, each the record of the account THEY ran it with. An org-wide read\n     narrowed by what the reader may see was considered and REJECTED - a row holds one real request\n     and one real response body of somebody's portal session, and no filter over an answer can un-read\n     that (docs/decisions.md, S2 review round 2026-08-22).\n  2. THE DEFINITION READ PREDICATE, which the collection ALONE cannot give and which is the whole\n     reason this is a module and not a route body. The two keys move INDEPENDENTLY: a row is addressed\n     by an action NAME and the package naming that action is a separate document, so a caller's OWN\n     rows outlive the definition that produced them - an action re-authored out of the package keeps\n     its row until it is superseded, discarded or aged out at 90 days, and getForActor answering a\n     reader their OWN private row before any org/global/baseline one silently narrows what they\n     resolve while their older rows stay put. Either way a straight collection read renders one real\n     request and one real response body beside an action the caller can no longer see, cannot run and\n     cannot name. So the definition is resolved FIRST, under the caller's own actor, through\n     resolveCapabilityDefinition (imported from the capability core, never re-derived), and only rows\n     whose action is on THAT definition survive.\n     recipe-store.listRecipesForActor re-filters by the same predicate for the same reason.\n  Suite: api/tests/security/action-evidence-view-isolation.test.ts (Rule 5 class).\n\nROUTE: GET /api/v1/integrations/:key/evidence, auth 'user', mounted BELOW the router-wide requireAuth\nblanket - and that POSITION is the admission. A gateway key never reaches it. Note what the blanket\nimplies for anyone editing this file: adding requireUserOrApiKey to a route below it is INERT (the\nblanket answers first), so making a route key-reachable means MOVING it above the blanket - which is\nexactly what integrations-capability.test.ts's stack walk catches, measured.\n\nNEW web modules (dashboard tier):\n  web/app/(dashboard)/integrations/[key]/page.tsx - the detail page. Renders the actions list, and per\n    action the read-only steps, the evidence sample, the run history, that action's schedules, run-now.\n  web/components/integrations/action-detail.tsx - one action, opened. Read-only by design: an action's\n    steps live on a definition document or on an automation, each with its own save gate, so this view\n    shows and links and never writes a step.\n  web/stores/integration-detail.ts - the page's ONE store, with FIVE failure channels (see\n    03-request-crud) and none of the state the list store already holds.\n  web/lib/integrations/action-view.ts - the ONE narrowing of the open `actions` record off the wire\n    (httpConfig -> method + URL template, automationBinding -> automationId). It reads DISPLAY detail\n    only and must never grow a second reading of `mutates`: backingType, requiresApproval, target and\n    approved all arrive already decided on IntegrationCapabilityAction.\n\nWHAT S2 DOES NOT ADD: no new collection, no new executor rail, no new write. The only write it can\ncause is run-now, which is the pre-existing POST .../execute and therefore meets C2's write gate\ninside executeUserIntegrationAction exactly as every other rail does.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-20 (slice S2, the integration DETAIL surface): one new read module on the api side,\nfour new modules on the web side, and no new state anywhere - S2 reads what S1 already records.\n\nNEW api/src/integrations/action-evidence-view.ts (tier 3) - the READ side of S1's\nintegration_action_evidence. A SEPARATE FILE from integration-capability.ts on purpose: that module is\nthe user-or-key capability core, and keeping them apart means nobody can make this collection\nkey-reachable by adding a field to a view the core already serves. It enforces TWO scopes:\n\n  1. THE ORG AND THE OWNER. S1's row is keyed by (orgId, ownerUserId, integrationKey, actionName) and\n     listForIntegration takes both identity terms; this module passes the CALLER'S OWN actor.orgId and\n     actor.userId, never an id read off a request. So neither another tenant's row nor a colleague's\n     is addressable at all. The consequence is deliberate: two members of one org see DIFFERENT\n     evidence for the same action, each the record of the account THEY ran it with. An org-wide read\n     narrowed by what the reader may see was considered and REJECTED - a row holds one real request\n     and one real response body of somebody's portal session, and no filter over an answer can un-read\n     that (docs/decisions.md, S2 review round 2026-08-22).\n  2. THE DEFINITION READ PREDICATE, which the collection ALONE cannot give and which is the whole\n     reason this is a module and not a route body. The two keys move INDEPENDENTLY: a row is addressed\n     by an action NAME and the package naming that action is a separate document, so a caller's OWN\n     rows outlive the definition that produced them - an action re-authored out of the package keeps\n     its row until it is superseded, discarded or aged out at 90 days, and getForActor answering a\n     reader their OWN private row before any org/global/baseline one silently narrows what they\n     resolve while their older rows stay put. Either way a straight collection read renders one real\n     request and one real response body beside an action the caller can no longer see, cannot run and\n     cannot name. So the definition is resolved FIRST, under the caller's own actor, through\n     resolveCapabilityDefinition (imported from the capability core, never re-derived), and only rows\n     whose action is on THAT definition survive.\n     recipe-store.listRecipesForActor re-filters by the same predicate for the same reason.\n  Suite: api/tests/security/action-evidence-view-isolation.test.ts (Rule 5 class).\n\nROUTE: GET /api/v1/integrations/:key/evidence, auth 'user', mounted BELOW the router-wide requireAuth\nblanket - and that POSITION is the admission. A gateway key never reaches it. Note what the blanket\nimplies for anyone editing this file: adding requireUserOrApiKey to a route below it is INERT (the\nblanket answers first), so making a route key-reachable means MOVING it above the blanket - which is\nexactly what integrations-capability.test.ts's stack walk catches, measured.\n\nNEW web modules (dashboard tier):\n  web/app/(dashboard)/integrations/[key]/page.tsx - the detail page. Renders the actions list, and per\n    action the read-only steps, the evidence sample, the run history, that action's schedules, run-now.\n  web/components/integrations/action-detail.tsx - one action, opened. Read-only by design: an action's\n    steps live on a definition document or on an automation, each with its own save gate, so this view\n    shows and links and never writes a step.\n  web/stores/integration-detail.ts - the page's ONE store, with FIVE failure channels (see\n    03-request-crud) and none of the state the list store already holds.\n  web/lib/integrations/action-view.ts - the ONE narrowing of the open `actions` record off the wire\n    (httpConfig -> method + URL template, automationBinding -> automationId). It reads DISPLAY detail\n    only and must never grow a second reading of `mutates`: backingType, requiresApproval, target and\n    approved all arrive already decided on IntegrationCapabilityAction.\n\nWHAT S2 DOES NOT ADD: no new collection, no new executor rail, no new write. The only write it can\ncause is run-now, which is the pre-existing POST .../execute and therefore meets C2's write gate\ninside executeUserIntegrationAction exactly as every other rail does.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-20 (slice S2, the integration DETAIL surface): one new read module on the api side,\nfour new modules on the web side, and no new state anywhere - S2 reads what S1 already records.\n\nNEW api/src/integrations/action-evidence-view.ts (tier 3) - the READ side of S1's\nintegration_action_evidence. A SEPARATE FILE from integration-capability.ts on purpose: that module is\nthe user-or-key capability core, and keeping them apart means nobody can make this collection\nkey-reachable by adding a field to a view the core already serves. It enforces TWO scopes:\n\n  1. THE ORG AND THE OWNER. S1's row is keyed by (orgId, ownerUserId, integrationKey, actionName) and\n     listForIntegration takes both identity terms; this module passes the CALLER'S OWN actor.orgId and\n     actor.userId, never an id read off a request. So neither another tenant's row nor a colleague's\n     is addressable at all. The consequence is deliberate: two members of one org see DIFFERENT\n     evidence for the same action, each the record of the account THEY ran it with. An org-wide read\n     narrowed by what the reader may see was considered and REJECTED - a row holds one real request\n     and one real response body of somebody's portal session, and no filter over an answer can un-read\n     that (docs/decisions.md, S2 review round 2026-08-22).\n  2. THE DEFINITION READ PREDICATE, which the collection ALONE cannot give and which is the whole\n     reason this is a module and not a route body. The two keys move INDEPENDENTLY: a row is addressed\n     by an action NAME and the package naming that action is a separate document, so a caller's OWN\n     rows outlive the definition that produced them - an action re-authored out of the package keeps\n     its row until it is superseded, discarded or aged out at 90 days, and getForActor answering a\n     reader their OWN private row before any org/global/baseline one silently narrows what they\n     resolve while their