      "height": 1067.5,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-24 (the capability-grant surface): THREE THIN ROUTES OVER MACHINERY THAT HAD NO\nCALLER. No new module, no new seam, no new collection. What changes is REACHABILITY - the same class\nof gap as the S6 publish doors, and the same shape of fix.\nJournal: docs/decisions.md 2026-08-24 \"the capability-grant surface is admin-gated, and lives on\nSettings/Devices\". Finding closed: capability-grants-have-no-route-or-ui...\n\nWHAT WAS UNREACHABLE. api/src/bridge/capability-grants.ts `grantCapability` / `revokeCapability`\nwere built and security-tested and called from NOTHING but those tests. The enforcement half was\nreal and correct - bridge/daemon-step-seam.ts refuses a step whose machine the org has not granted,\ndefault-deny, checked BEFORE any credential is delivered - so with no way to WRITE a grant,\ndesktop.automation and local.bash could never be turned on and every browser and bash step in\nproduction refused forever. The module's own docblock says \"only the tenant can answer\" the grant\nquestion; nothing in the product asked it.\n\napi/src/bridge/capability-grants.ts  (existing module, NEW exports - no new file)\n  orgMachines(orgId)                 the fleet READ: advertised (registry) beside granted (grants),\n                                     one row per machine. Org-scoped by construction - it composes\n                                     advertisedCapabilitiesForOrg, which filters on the org and\n                                     drops revoked pairings, so a foreign machine is not a row.\n  machineForOrg(orgId, pairingId)    one machine, NULL for unknown AND for another tenant's alike,\n                                     so a caller turning null into a 404 is not an existence oracle.\n  grantedEgressEndpoint(...)         the ADDRESS a live egress.residential grant authorises. Read\n                                     separately because a surface showing capability NAMES alone\n                                     cannot show what is actually being decided (registry.ts).\n  grantCapabilityAudited(...)        write + Registo row as ONE operation\n  revokeCapabilityAudited(...)       same, and audited even when nothing was live to turn off\n  NEW INTRA-MODULE EDGE: capability-grants.ts -> registry.ts (advertisedCapabilitiesForOrg,\n  getPairingById, isLive). Acyclic: registry.ts reads the grants STORE directly and does not import\n  capability-grants.ts. No module boundary is crossed - both are api/src/bridge/.\n\napi/src/bridge/index.ts  - the five above join isCapabilityGranted on the module's public face,\n  because routes/ may not import data/ (ch02 2.7) and the audit write is a data/ write.\n\napi/src/routes/bridge.ts  (existing router, THREE new routes)\n  GET    /api/v1/bridge/machines\n  POST   /api/v1/bridge/pairings/:pairingId/capabilities\n  DELETE /api/v1/bridge/pairings/:pairingId/capabilities/:capability\n  All auth: 'org-admin' - the CONTRACT-LEVEL TIER MARKING (api-contract.md CONV-1 lists\n  super-admin/org-admin as \"marked per endpoint\"), naming the NARROWEST role admitted, exactly as\n  registo.listRegisto declares org-admin over a router gated on both. Each route mounts\n  requireRole('org-admin', 'super-admin'); BOTH roles are named because requireRole is an exact\n  membership test and does not treat super-admin as a superset. THE ROLE CHECK IS MIDDLEWARE, so it\n  runs before any store read, and that ordering is the security property: a non-admin's 403 is\n  identical for a machine in their org, one in another org and one that does not exist, so the\n  refusal is not itself an oracle. Only callers past it reach the org-scoped lookup, whose 404 is\n  then byte-identical for unknown and foreign. (First cut declared auth:'user' with the check\n  hand-rolled in each handler - a contract-accuracy defect on a privilege-widening surface,\n  corrected in the same unmerged commit; see docs/decisions.md D3 REVIEW ROUND.)\n\n  NOT the owner-or-admin rule of DELETE /pairings/:pairingId beside it. Revoking a pairing is an\n  emergency anyone must be able to perform on their own hardware; granting WIDENS what the org's\n  work may run on, so it belongs to whoever answers for the tenant. A user who could grant their own\n  laptop local.bash would be authorising their own computer to execute the org's automations.\n\nweb/  - stores/bridge-machines.ts (NEW) + components/settings/paired-machines-section.tsx (NEW),\n  rendered on the EXISTING /settings/devices page and gated on role. That page existed and was\n  reachable only by the URL the bridge CLI prints, so it joins the settings nav here. Every\n  mutation replaces the machine row from the SERVER's answer: a grant can be stored in a form the\n  client did not send (the endpoint is canonicalised server-side) and can be recorded for a\n  capability the machine does not advertise, so an optimistic local row would show an administrator\n  a grant that reads as effective while authorising nothing.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-24 (the capability-grant surface): THREE THIN ROUTES OVER MACHINERY THAT HAD NO\nCALLER. No new module, no new seam, no new collection. What changes is REACHABILITY - the same class\nof gap as the S6 publish doors, and the same shape of fix.\nJournal: docs/decisions.md 2026-08-24 \"the capability-grant surface is admin-gated, and lives on\nSettings/Devices\". Finding closed: capability-grants-have-no-route-or-ui...\n\nWHAT WAS UNREACHABLE. api/src/bridge/capability-grants.ts `grantCapability` / `revokeCapability`\nwere built and security-tested and called from NOTHING but those tests. The enforcement half was\nreal and correct - bridge/daemon-step-seam.ts refuses a step whose machine the org has not granted,\ndefault-deny, checked BEFORE any credential is delivered - so with no way to WRITE a grant,\ndesktop.automation and local.bash could never be turned on and every browser and bash step in\nproduction refused forever. The module's own docblock says \"only the tenant can answer\" the grant\nquestion; nothing in the product asked it.\n\napi/src/bridge/capability-grants.ts  (existing module, NEW exports - no new file)\n  orgMachines(orgId)                 the fleet READ: advertised (registry) beside granted (grants),\n                                     one row per machine. Org-scoped by construction - it composes\n                                     advertisedCapabilitiesForOrg, which filters on the org and\n                                     drops revoked pairings, so a foreign machine is not a row.\n  machineForOrg(orgId, pairingId)    one machine, NULL for unknown AND for another tenant's alike,\n                                     so a caller turning null into a 404 is not an existence oracle.\n  grantedEgressEndpoint(...)         the ADDRESS a live egress.residential grant authorises. Read\n                                     separately because a surface showing capability NAMES alone\n                                     cannot show what is actually being decided (registry.ts).\n  grantCapabilityAudited(...)        write + Registo row as ONE operation\n  revokeCapabilityAudited(...)       same, and audited even when nothing was live to turn off\n  NEW INTRA-MODULE EDGE: capability-grants.ts -> registry.ts (advertisedCapabilitiesForOrg,\n  getPairingById, isLive). Acyclic: registry.ts reads the grants STORE directly and does not import\n  capability-grants.ts. No module boundary is crossed - both are api/src/bridge/.\n\napi/src/bridge/index.ts  - the five above join isCapabilityGranted on the module's public face,\n  because routes/ may not import data/ (ch02 2.7) and the audit write is a data/ write.\n\napi/src/routes/bridge.ts  (existing router, THREE new routes)\n  GET    /api/v1/bridge/machines\n  POST   /api/v1/bridge/pairings/:pairingId/capabilities\n  DELETE /api/v1/bridge/pairings/:pairingId/capabilities/:capability\n  All auth: 'org-admin' - the CONTRACT-LEVEL TIER MARKING (api-contract.md CONV-1 lists\n  super-admin/org-admin as \"marked per endpoint\"), naming the NARROWEST role admitted, exactly as\n  registo.listRegisto declares org-admin over a router gated on both. Each route mounts\n  requireRole('org-admin', 'super-admin'); BOTH roles are named because requireRole is an exact\n  membership test and does not treat super-admin as a superset. THE ROLE CHECK IS MIDDLEWARE, so it\n  runs before any store read, and that ordering is the security property: a non-admin's 403 is\n  identical for a machine in their org, one in another org and one that does not exist, so the\n  refusal is not itself an oracle. Only callers past it reach the org-scoped lookup, whose 404 is\n  then byte-identical for unknown and foreign. (First cut declared auth:'user' with the check\n  hand-rolled in each handler - a contract-accuracy defect on a privilege-widening surface,\n  corrected in the same unmerged commit; see docs/decisions.md D3 REVIEW ROUND.)\n\n  NOT the owner-or-admin rule of DELETE /pairings/:pairingId beside it. Revoking a pairing is an\n  emergency anyone must be able to perform on their own hardware; granting WIDENS what the org's\n  work may run on, so it belongs to whoever answers for the tenant. A user who could grant their own\n  laptop local.bash would be authorising their own computer to execute the org's automations.\n\nweb/  - stores/bridge-machines.ts (NEW) + components/settings/paired-machines-section.tsx (NEW),\n  rendered on the EXISTING /settings/devices page and gated on role. That page existed and was\n  reachable only by the URL the bridge CLI prints, so it joins the settings nav here. Every\n  mutation replaces the machine row from the SERVER's answer: a grant can be stored in a form the\n  client did not send (the endpoint is canonicalised server-side) and can be recorded for a\n  capability the machine does not advertise, so an optimistic local row would show an administrator\n  a grant that reads as effective while authorising nothing.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-24 (the capability-grant surface): THREE THIN ROUTES OVER MACHINERY THAT HAD NO\nCALLER. No new module, no new seam, no new collection. What changes is REACHABILITY - the same class\nof gap as the S6 publish doors, and the same shape of fix.\nJournal: docs/decisions.md 2026-08-24 \"the capability-grant surface is admin-gated, and lives on\nSettings/Devices\". Finding closed: capability-grants-have-no-route-or-ui...\n\nWHAT WAS UNREACHABLE. api/src/bridge/capability-grants.ts `grantCapability` / `revokeCapability`\nwere built and security-tested and called from NOTHING but those tests. The enforcement half was\nreal and correct - bridge/daemon-step-seam.ts refuses a step whose machine the org has not granted,\ndefault-deny, checked BEFORE any credential is delivered - so with no way to WRITE a grant,\ndesktop.automation and local.bash could never be turned on and every browser and bash step in\nproduction refused forever. The module's own docblock says \"only the tenant can answer\" the grant\nquestion; nothing in the product asked it.\n\napi/src/bridge/capability-grants.ts  (existing module, NEW exports - no new file)\n  orgMachines(orgId)                 the fleet READ: advertised (registry) beside granted (grants),\n                                     one row per machine. Org-scoped by construction - it composes\n                                     advertisedCapabilitiesForOrg, which filters on the org and\n                                     drops revoked pairings, so a foreign machine is not a row.\n  machineForOrg(orgId, pairingId)    one machine, NULL for unknown AND for another tenant's alike,\n                                     so a caller turning null into a 404 is not an existence oracle.\n  grantedEgressEndpoint(...)         the ADDRESS a live egress.residential grant authorises. Read\n                                     separately because a surface showing capability NAMES alone\n                                     cannot show what is actually being decided (registry.ts).\n  grantCapabilityAudited(...)        write + Registo row as ONE operation\n  revokeCapabilityAudited(...)       same, and audited even when nothing was live to turn off\n  NEW INTRA-MODULE EDGE: capability-grants.ts -> registry.ts (advertisedCapabilitiesForOrg,\n  getPairingById, isLive). Acyclic: registry.ts reads the grants STORE directly and does not import\n  capability-grants.ts. No module boundary is crossed - both are api/src/bridge/.\n\napi/src/bridge/index.ts  - the five above join isCapabilityGranted on the module's public face,\n  because routes/ may not import data/ (ch02 2.7) and the audit write is a data/ write.\n\napi/src/routes/bridge.ts  (existing router, THREE new routes)\n  GET    /api/v1/bridge/machines\n  POST   /api/v1/bridge/pairings/:pairingId/capabilities\n  DELETE /api/v1/bridge/pairings/:pairingId/capabilities/:capability\n  All auth: 'org-admin' - the CONTRACT-LEVEL TIER MARKING (api-contract.md CONV-1 lists\n  super-admin/org-admin as \"marked per endpoint\"), naming the NARROWEST role admitted, exactly as\n  registo.listRegisto declares org-admin over a router gated on both. Each route mounts\n  requireRole('org-admin', 'super-admin'); BOTH roles are named because requireRole is an exact\n  membership test and does not treat super-admin as a superset. THE ROLE CHECK IS MIDDLEWARE, so it\n  runs before any store read, and that ordering is the security property: a non-admin's 403 is\n  identical for a machine in their org, one in another org and one that does not exist, so the\n  refusal is not itself an oracle. Only callers past it reach the org-scoped lookup, whose 404 is\n  then byte-identical for unknown and foreign. (First cut declared auth:'user' with the check\n  hand-rolled in each handler - a contract-accuracy defect on a privilege-widening surface,\n  corrected in the same unmerged commit; see docs/decisions.md D3 REVIEW ROUND.)\n\n  NOT the owner-or-admin rule of DELETE /pairings/:pairingId beside it. Revoking a pairing is an\n  emergency anyone must be able to perform on their own hardware; granting WIDENS what the org's\n  work may run on, so it belongs to whoever answers for the tenant. A user who could grant their own\n  laptop local.bash would be authorising their own computer to execute the org's automations.\n\nweb/  - stores/bridge-machines.ts (NEW) + components/settings/paired-machines-section.tsx (NEW),\n  rendered on the EXISTING /settings/devices page and gated on role. That page existed and was\n  reachable only by the URL the bridge CLI prints, so it joins the settings nav here. Every\n  mutation replaces the machine row from the SERVER's answer: a grant can be stored in a form the\n  client did not send (the endpoint is canonicalised server-side) and can be recorded for a\n  capability the machine does not advertise, so an optimistic local row would show an administrator\n  a grant that reads as effective while authorising nothing.",
